{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic","id":705402,"title":"Vampires in the Neolithic","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-10-17T10:43:20-07:00","id":705402,"title":"Vampires in the Neolithic","content":"I've been pondering about vampires back in that age, with far smaller population numbers to draw on. A single village would likely only house one or two vampires, and if another tried moving in, it'd likely be very brutal.<br /> <br /> So vampires would face themselves as threats, and have to be extremely careful in their feeding to not draw suspicion. Without the heavy politics and scheming of modern day nights though, what sort of challenges would a vampire face, long term, beyond those already listed?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2650-retias","name":"Retias","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"192"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-17T12:38:49-07:00","id":705632,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I'd definitely expect that larger populations of kindred would be rare if at all present. I'd expect to see more open and symbiotic relationships between kindred and humans. I'd picture cults of worship. I'd picture warring tribes where the vampire lends his people unprecedented destruction on the battlefield and the path of conquest lends him plenty of vitae. I'd picture more nomadic kindred forced to conserve their energy and strike at varying settlements from afar to avoid getting hunted down.<br /> <br /> If I were doing a neolithic vampire game, I'd probably lean towards a barbarian fantasy angle. Mages and werewolves would probably feature prominently as threats. It would almost certainly necessitate a game where the character/s are forced to travel, because, well, the stone age village in the middle of nowhere likely wouldn't be an enthralling setting for very long.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-17T12:56:53-07:00","id":705640,"title":"","content":"I think the biggest obstacle vampires would face in any past era would be the prevalent use of open flame for just about everything, a real and immediate problem when your beast has a tendency to go nuts whenever it encounters the flickering yellow tongue.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-17T13:21:42-07:00","id":705650,"title":"","content":"Here's a big one - with the human population so small and spread out, what's a cave-vamp to do when a plague sweeps through the only village for days in any direction and threatens to wipe out most of the people? It may not even leave enough alive to maintain a vampire population at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8485-random_interrupt","name":"Random_Interrupt","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8485_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-17T13:37:49-07:00","id":705680,"title":"","content":"Eat animals, adapt to devour ghosts or spirits or fall into torpor.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-17T15:00:02-07:00","id":705748,"title":"","content":"I think I recall the writers say the vampires in the neolithic were mostly just Strix and similar possesed corpses, nothing like the Kindred and their Clans yet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-17T20:11:45-07:00","id":706161,"title":"","content":"Three words: Undead God-Kings","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://eoinsstuff.shoutwiki.com/wiki/Genius:_the_Transgression_(2nd_Edition)\" target=\"_blank\">Genius: the Transgression 2E is a thing that's being worked on.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4488-malonkey1","name":"malonkey1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4488_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-17T21:05:39-07:00","id":706267,"title":"","content":"Well, I run VtM as my Vampire for my WoD, but, during the Neolithic I pretty much have vampires as exactly that, mostly God-Kings with only a vampire and his small brood per group of people. Most of the time, Vampires are territorial, and tend to have lots of territory fights. Though, since I include the Strix too, they pose a lot of problems for the smaller Kindred population.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><u><b>My Homebrew</b></u><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1PLu_eTg03h4ogiTSGSkzEB7T_1YpB9_VkhHmgZN41-I/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Star Wars d10</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">T</a><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">he Fallen for Demon: the Descent</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-TDr4NF_5cUn-In2vjI9P5LTKqsu9B4-OMxbnbaTgT8/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">Requiem for a Masquerade: VtR 2e Cainite Conversion</a> (<a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HpKi5giBNsQrZRLIRRjaCe7sHVm1Q13ravVYlE45Dbw/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Vampira Roma</a>)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1601-dreaminggod","name":"Dreaminggod","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"745"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-18T20:35:02-07:00","id":708094,"title":"","content":"Would an isolated vampire, one that tried to follow hunter-gatherers, be able to eke out an existence if the local population was thick enough? Because I feel that unless you <i>do </i>go all the way to Undead God-Kings, trying to dominate small villages with your Disciplines would be so <i>very very exposed</i>.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-18T21:47:41-07:00","id":708131,"title":"","content":"I imagine each vampire picked how they did it. Gangrel, for instance, following hunter-gathers, and feeding on animals. If you suppose a tribe of 50 or so people, you could survive on them until you couldn't subsist on human blood.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><u><b>My Homebrew</b></u><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1PLu_eTg03h4ogiTSGSkzEB7T_1YpB9_VkhHmgZN41-I/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Star Wars d10</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">T</a><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">he Fallen for Demon: the Descent</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-TDr4NF_5cUn-In2vjI9P5LTKqsu9B4-OMxbnbaTgT8/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">Requiem for a Masquerade: VtR 2e Cainite Conversion</a> (<a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HpKi5giBNsQrZRLIRRjaCe7sHVm1Q13ravVYlE45Dbw/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Vampira Roma</a>)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1601-dreaminggod","name":"Dreaminggod","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"745"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-18T22:14:26-07:00","id":708150,"title":"","content":"Dave or Chris Allen mentioned the Strix-possessed corpses thing. I think that suggests the Clans might not have solidified yet, and it's likely that a lot of vampires are Strix/those THINGS they may have made via synthesis and such (i.e. normal vampires, or something that roughly resembles them)<br /> <br /> I could see a like, 3-4 of these proto-vampires coming together to hunt humans, and that being a common tactic. Their Humanity probably doesn't last long, so there'd be more Draugr than normal Kindred, IMO.<br /> <br /> They probably will pick off a single person and split the meal between each other. Maybe they get greedier when they become draugr and split off. They mentioned in general that people at the time know not to wander off at night away from others, and these vampire creatures are probably one of many reasons you don't do that.<br /> <br /> If they do it too much, they'll kill their food source. Or that food source might wise up and hunt THEM down. It'd be very brutal stuff, I imagine. <br /> <br /> I imagine that vampire culture and identity comes a bit later, with agriculture. Can't you see the parallels between keeping a proper herd and farming and domestication and such? It actually dovetails pretty nicely with neolithic mage and werewolf with this kind of communal grouping changing the dynamic for that splat.<br /> <br /> IMO, for those vampires that do wise up, they'd probably be something like the Weihan Cynn describes, ancestors that the humans of their tribe fearfully pay respects to, in exchange for their protection.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-18T23:26:02-07:00","id":708271,"title":"","content":"I think the idea that vampires as we understand them don't exist in the neolithic era fits well with the lore. Kindred arose in tandem with the rise of proper civilization and are a parasitic reflection of that growth. It's probably the route I'd take, but I could see early versions of the clans existing if someone wanted to take a different route. Here's how I'd see them fitting in.<br /> <br /> Nosferatu:For generations, they'd walk out into the wilderness. It was a dream-plague. They'd speak of nightmares, visions growing in intensity until finally, in the dead of night, they'd venture out barefoot. The villagers would later find their corpses strewn out, drained of blood. In desperation, they turned to sacrifice. Once a season the weakest among them is led out to the caves no one dares venture into. For now, this seems to placate the Beast.<br /> <br /> Mekhet: It's a region more advanced than most, blessed with fertile land, supporting a strip of settlements with maintained but minimal contact. When they do communicate, it's with whispers of a shared specter, a creature that walks among the shadows. It can appear in any home, and while bodies are sometimes found, most just bless the fact that these incidences are rare, that those who suffer the night visitations are lucky to wake up in a weakened state rather than dead.<br /> <br /> Ventrue: For generations, their slaughter goddess has brought them prosperity. While other tribes are forced to toil over their livestock, they merely take what they desire. They pour into villages in the dead of night, confident in the knowledge that the crude tools of their enemies have little power over their queen. That she limits their use of fire in the dead of night is a small price to pay. They've learned to adapt, to see better in the dark than they should be capable of, and the best of them are blessed with strength no mere mortal should possess.<br /> <br /> Daeva: They share in her blood, and she in theirs, and this is the way it's been for as long as they can remember. Those who have tried to make contact with them whisper of their lunacy, of fanatic blood orgies, and those few who have dared come with arms were sent back in pieces.<br /> <br /> Gangrel: Their livestock goes missing. This is hardly unusually, merely the price of life in early civilization. Yet these animals don't represent attacks by any predators they know. Yet, sometimes farmers go missing. They've sent out parties before, armed with makeshift spears and torches. Most nights they've seen nothing, but occasionally they'll catch sight of a creature that walks like a men but has the face of a beast. For a while, the killings abate, but whatever it is, it always returns.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-18T23:41:45-07:00","id":708294,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708150#post708150\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I imagine that vampire culture and identity comes a bit later, with agriculture. Can't you see the parallels between keeping a proper herd and farming and domestication and such?</div> </div> </div> </div>Better still- imagine in their wonderful splat-centric ignorance, a neonate approaches his sire and asks &quot;how long have we been doing this&quot;, and the sire grins predatorily and answers &quot;mankind used to dwell in tiny groups, surrounded by darkness. <i>They learned better</i>&quot;. It makes for a good blurb about Ventrue and the birth of animal husbandry, if nothing else, yeah?<br /> <br /> I could totally see a vampire game with little other splat presence having kindred that claim that proto-vampires manipulated humans into large groups, then cities, for their population densIII MEAN so that humans would be safe from other prehistoric animals and supernatuals. And <i>thus </i>increase human population density. Pretty demoralizing, knowing that your species' big break only happened because a bunch of ambulatory corpses were tired of getting up off their pale, lazy asses and hunting like nature intended. Or is the whole thing a little too Masquerade?<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1289,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","user":"Hastur00","date":". 10-19-2015, 02:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T07:13:30-07:00","id":708721,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hastur00</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708294#post708294\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or is the whole thing a little too Masquerade?<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>It depends. If it's accurate, yes. If the Ventrue are just lying about it and even believe it themselves despite it being factually incorrect, no.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T07:18:09-07:00","id":708722,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hastur00</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708294#post708294\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Better still- imagine in their wonderful splat-centric ignorance, a neonate approaches his sire and asks &quot;how long have we been doing this&quot;, and the sire grins predatorily and answers &quot;mankind used to dwell in tiny groups, surrounded by darkness. <i>They learned better</i>&quot;. It makes for a good blurb about Ventrue and the birth of animal husbandry, if nothing else, yeah?<br /> <br /> I could totally see a vampire game with little other splat presence having kindred that claim that proto-vampires manipulated humans into large groups, then cities, for their population densIII MEAN so that humans would be safe from other prehistoric animals and supernatuals. And <i>thus </i>increase human population density. Pretty demoralizing, knowing that your species' big break only happened because a bunch of ambulatory corpses were tired of getting up off their pale, lazy asses and hunting like nature intended. Or is the whole thing a little too Masquerade?<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>One of my gripes with Requiem is that it stands alone in the nWoD as the game where any amount of lore is considered <i>too Masquerade</i>. Games are meant to have lore, and nWoD (including Requiem, as seen from the fanbase's response to the clanbooks) learned that lesson a long time ago.<br /> <br /> It just feels that Requiem exists solely in Year 0 and Rome. Anything before or after that is, more or less, a variation of the existing clans plus the existing covenants. Prehistoric Strix-ridden corpses just feels <i>lazy</i> and <i>unplayable</i> as an explanation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T07:26:05-07:00","id":708726,"title":"","content":"I think the Ancient books, as well as the clan books, go a long way towards building the lore and keeping it completely different from Masquerade. There's a lot of lore in vampire. The main thing that keeps it distinguished from Masquerade is that it is not all-encompassing of all vampires, nor is it all known or agreed upon.<br /> <br /> The idea that neolithic vampires created human civilization is something a vampire in Requiem would totally imply, if not outright state. The more arrogant might even claim to be descended from the vampires that did it, while the more savvy would wave that off as statistically unlikely while also, cannily, implying that they <i>might </i>be.<br /> <br /> But for the most part, new World of Darkness sticks to more low-key and realistic depictions of things. If there were groups of vampires, they would be scattered and disparate and unorganized. In one place they may manage to pull things together and get a blood farm going, in others a vampire might try it only to find that the humans left while they slept. Just like one group of people didn't create agriculture and spread across the globe instilling it into others, one group of vampires wouldn't create static settlements and travel around trapping humans with it. It's the kind of thing that would require insane amounts of resources and foresight, as well as motivation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 10-19-2015, 10:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T07:32:19-07:00","id":708728,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708722#post708722\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of my gripes with Requiem is that it stands alone in the nWoD as the game where any amount of lore is considered <i>too Masquerade</i>. Games are meant to have lore, and nWoD (including Requiem, as seen from the fanbase's response to the clanbooks) learned that lesson a long time ago.<br /> <br /> It just feels that Requiem exists solely in Year 0 and Rome. Anything before or after that is, more or less, a variation of the existing clans plus the existing covenants. Prehistoric Strix-ridden corpses just feels <i>lazy</i> and <i>unplayable</i> as an explanation.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Where I play with a Synthesis of both, I'm unburdened by things being too '<i>Masquerade</i>'. On that front, however, I find Requiem's lore to be interesting in it's own right. However, I find myself preferring parts from both, so have endeavored to merge them. I would say Requiem doesn't solely in Year 0 or Rome. Ancient Mysteries had some variations on the Five, while VtR 2.0 added lost Clans and Covenants, bringing the core Clan's to above 5.<br /> <br /> As my personal Vampire includes Strix, having many of the vampire legends be Strix possessed corpses fits, and I certainly use it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><u><b>My Homebrew</b></u><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1PLu_eTg03h4ogiTSGSkzEB7T_1YpB9_VkhHmgZN41-I/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Star Wars d10</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">T</a><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">he Fallen for Demon: the Descent</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-TDr4NF_5cUn-In2vjI9P5LTKqsu9B4-OMxbnbaTgT8/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">Requiem for a Masquerade: VtR 2e Cainite Conversion</a> (<a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HpKi5giBNsQrZRLIRRjaCe7sHVm1Q13ravVYlE45Dbw/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Vampira Roma</a>)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1601-dreaminggod","name":"Dreaminggod","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"745"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T08:09:26-07:00","id":708750,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dreaminggod</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708728#post708728\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Where I play with a Synthesis of both, I'm unburdened by things being too '<i>Masquerade</i>'. On that front, however, I find Requiem's lore to be interesting in it's own right. However, I find myself preferring parts from both, so have endeavored to merge them. I would say Requiem doesn't solely in Year 0 or Rome. Ancient Mysteries had some variations on the Five, while VtR 2.0 added lost Clans and Covenants, bringing the core Clan's to above 5.<br /> <br /> As my personal Vampire includes Strix, having many of the vampire legends be Strix possessed corpses fits, and I certainly use it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agree with you on the synthesis, I just meant to say that going back to simplistic explanations would be a step back from VtR 2nd edition's lost clans and covenants. If only the Strix existed back then, I'd love some hints as to how they operated and around when and how did the first Kindred pop up.<br /> <br /> I make my observations based on forum activity (lore topic vs some minor rule homebrew topics) and the scale is tipped all the way to the latter for most of the time. That's not asking for a more Masquerade-like Requiem, it's just comparing Requiem to games with richer lore (Warhammer 40K rpg). Also, I don't think it's healthier that we went from the dark superheroes of Masquerade to the humans with fangs (just another personal observation that I accept might not be the case). I would argue &quot;to each his own&quot; if I was interested in either of those two scenarios, but what about those of us, like you and me, who want Vampires, plain and simple. Not superheros, nor humans with fangs who act like <b>Morality</b> 8 characters all the way down to Humanity 2? I think in both cases the lore was catalytic in giving rise to these two different styles of play.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T08:09:34-07:00","id":708751,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708722#post708722\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Prehistoric Strix-ridden corpses just feels <i>lazy</i></div> </div> </div> </div>Or a logical extrapolation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">and <i>unplayable</i></div> </div> </div> </div>Not every group is amenable to being played in every context or time period.<br /> <br /> I mean, part of the appeal being built around this whole Neolithic thing is that it looks as close as one can get to what we know of the Neolithic if we account for a present (but still somewhat separated) spirit world, and that includes population numbers and models that really don't do much to accommodate a bunch of the Vampire staples. Although really, even if one does make dramatic adjustments... exactly how playable is vampire god-kings anyway? What are you going to do with it in a session of 2-5 people? It sounds more detailed than the more sensible (and equally sparse of play options) idea of vampires as lurkers in the dark who occasionally stole into communities to prey on individuals (I don't think secrecy is too much of a concern, because people have different attitudes and a small number of them might not get through your primeval Majesty or whatever), but what do you actually do with it?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T08:53:51-07:00","id":708796,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708751#post708751\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Or a logical extrapolation.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Not every group is amenable to being played in every context or time period.<br /> <br /> I mean, part of the appeal being built around this whole Neolithic thing is that it looks as close as one can get to what we know of the Neolithic if we account for a present (but still somewhat separated) spirit world, and that includes population numbers and models that really don't do much to accommodate a bunch of the Vampire staples. Although really, even if one does make dramatic adjustments... exactly how playable is vampire god-kings anyway? What are you going to do with it in a session of 2-5 people? It sounds more detailed than the more sensible (and equally sparse of play options) idea of vampires as lurkers in the dark who occasionally stole into communities to prey on individuals (I don't think secrecy is too much of a concern, because people have different attitudes and a small number of them might not get through your primeval Majesty or whatever), but what do you actually do with it?</div> </div> </div> </div>You're not wrong, and if blood gods exist in prehistorical times they are more likely to be Strix-possessed (willingly or otherwise) than anything else. My issue here is that we're not getting more answers (or stories or myths) here, since the Fog of Eternity was removed, but the same amount as we did in the past. The explanation seems to be &quot;Vampires are selfish and lie and no truth can survive that&quot; amidst a sea of chronicles wherein Vampires are humans with fangs against an ethical backdrop way more pleasant than even reality (seriously, in a world of shadowy corporate treaties and a near-universal application of inequality measures in favor of the rich, how can there not be undead manipulators behind everything?).<br /> <br /> Have we even realize we have a year from now until corporations will sue governments in secret courts for raising safety and health standards, switching to free software, or raising the minimum wage? You might be in favor of such reforms, but you have to admit that's as close to cyberpunk: the beginning, as it gets. In a world where my European ISP will hand my private information to multinational studios so they can sue me for millions for watching an episode of Simpsons on pop corn time, the World of Darkness won't look so dark after a while.<br /> <br /> I have more than enough books and pdfs that detail an extremely long and interesting part of or time in the lore <b>by a bullet point story hook</b> (that's my main issue and argument here) or a mix and twist of the 5 + 5. All it took for the Movement to rise was a flyer by some random French vampire, not the centuries of scientific research and intense, violent debates that the mortal Left actually experienced. Yes, that is different from the Anarchs of Masquerade, but it is sinfully simplistic.<br /> <br /> It feels like we're repeating mantras in an algorithmic fashion. Would x make sense? Yes. Then protest that Requiem is not Masquerade. If the Requiem != Masquerade code fails, we resort to claims of realism and logic.<br /> <br /> I realize this is a long post covering issues not related to the topic here. Just my two cents, on how I tend to mix VtM and VtR, and how that ultimately makes players of both lines uncomfortable sitting at my table. (Where are the Lasombra in your game? Why is your WoD so bleak?).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T09:39:42-07:00","id":708872,"title":"","content":"It's more that we're not getting answers because it's not Vampire in the Neolithic Age that got an era. If more people voted for Vampire eras during the Dark Eras polls, we would have gotten more expanded-upon timelines. Vampire showed up 9 times with four different eras and didn't win any of them, eventually getting rescued by a backer to mesh them with Promethean.<br /> <br /> But Dark Eras still has the Requiem for Regina.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 10-19-2015, 12:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T10:25:45-07:00","id":708969,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708722#post708722\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of my gripes with Requiem is that it stands alone in the nWoD as the game where any amount of lore is considered <i>too Masquerade</i>. Games are meant to have lore, and nWoD (including Requiem, as seen from the fanbase's response to the clanbooks) learned that lesson a long time ago.<br /> <br /> It just feels that Requiem exists solely in Year 0 and Rome. Anything before or after that is, more or less, a variation of the existing clans plus the existing covenants. Prehistoric Strix-ridden corpses just feels <i>lazy</i> and <i>unplayable</i> as an explanation.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I don't know. I think you're faulting the game for being what it is. VtR is supposed to be insular and claustrophobic. It's a game where you're parasites in a city, and that city is your entire ecosystem. It's built as a setting where you play an immortal who's use of that immortality is limited by the bane of the sun, where knowledge, mobility, and power are limited by the lack of feasibility of the innate limitations that come with being a walking corpse.. It's fine if you don't like that setting, but at its core, but its fundamental design limits the impact of vast lore on the day-to-day workings of a game. I actually find it pretty refreshing that the supplements are generally modular tool kits rather than expansions of the mythology.<br /> <br /> I don't think a prehistoric setting is all that playable in VtR without a radical shift in what the game is about. They're urban predators, a cynical reflection of our own society, and that doesn't really work in a pre-society setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T10:35:34-07:00","id":708981,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708751#post708751\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Or a logical extrapolation.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Not every group is amenable to being played in every context or time period.<br /> <br /> I mean, part of the appeal being built around this whole Neolithic thing is that it looks as close as one can get to what we know of the Neolithic if we account for a present (but still somewhat separated) spirit world, and that includes population numbers and models that really don't do much to accommodate a bunch of the Vampire staples. Although really, even if one does make dramatic adjustments... exactly how playable is vampire god-kings anyway? What are you going to do with it in a session of 2-5 people? It sounds more detailed than the more sensible (and equally sparse of play options) idea of vampires as lurkers in the dark who occasionally stole into communities to prey on individuals (I don't think secrecy is too much of a concern, because people have different attitudes and a small number of them might not get through your primeval Majesty or whatever), but what do you actually do with it?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah, I'm having a hard time coming up with a vision of Neolithic Vampire that would actually be compelling. The idea of vampire god-kings is compelling, but what do they even do with their time? They have these herds of simple farmers or hunter-gatherers and, well, generally not much else at all. Kindred are parasites. They're reflective of the society they live in, and if that society doesn't amount to much, you're not going to have a lot of material to work with.<br /> <br /> I could potentially see a Mage or Werewolf game working, since they still have this rich world of spirits to interact with, and I could see a Vampire God-King making a great antagonist in that milieu. I could also see a more experimental game where players command a tribe trying to survive amidst all the natural and supernatural horrors around them, but straight vampire just seems like a poor fit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T10:38:11-07:00","id":708985,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708722#post708722\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">One of my gripes with Requiem is that it stands alone in the nWoD as the game where any amount of lore is considered <i>too Masquerade</i>. </span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Seconded. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708726#post708726\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If there were groups of vampires, they would be scattered and disparate and unorganized. In one place they may manage to pull things together and get a blood farm going, in others a vampire might try it only to find that the humans left while they slept. Just like one group of people didn't create agriculture and spread across the globe instilling it into others, one group of vampires wouldn't create static settlements and travel around trapping humans with it. It's the kind of thing that would require insane amounts of <b>resources </b>and <b>foresight</b>, as well as <b>motivation</b>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe not, but it makes a good story. Hell, it makes a good <i>legend</i>. Who has more of the bolded qualities than a centuries-old proto-vampire who's tired of living night to night on the whims of disease, predation by megafauna, weather, war... worse still, if they're social with humans, they can only do so briefly and transiently, or else by bringing humanity to them.<br /> <br /> Hell of a tale, huh? The earliest, oldest vampires, realizing their physical vulnerability and psychological toll of solitude, began to draw mankind together; first in isolated spots. The monsters hear legends of other frightening nighttime blood-stealers, and first contact is made, probably by a warrior determined to protect his monopoly. They can't <i>all </i>kill each other, though, and over time repeated contact breeds a squint-eyed, same-basket trust. Much more alarming is (re)discovering the bittersweet joy of being part of a community, even if that community is other untrustworthy monsters.<br /> <br /> So. Against all logic vampires find themselves to be hunters trying to integrate with prey, and each other, because it turns out that's the price of damnation. Having been born into an environment where scarcity of resources is a law of nature, the new kindred would immediately see what would be needed to congregate closer, into these newfangled <i>cities </i>that have been in all the papers. Some use their acquired knowledge of how animals change with environmental pressures , and animal husbandry is born (much easier when you can <i>talk </i>to your stock). Some follow the nomads, and ensure that they'll follow the best game, and innocently let slip of a place where that game can be traded for goods. Some take their ability to see patterns and predict the future to learn how to alter native plants to the humans' best advantage, and once those humans are all in the same place, new social patterns become raw data for an eventual legal infrastructure. Of course, not everybody's on board, so some have to go out and be... convincing. Make sure that enough gatherers are supporting the new settlement with resources, and once the settlement is stable and provided for, making sure that new social frictions don't tear it into factions, perhaps by creating activities that are universally enjoyable (and distracting). And of course, the... nightmare enforcers. Remember the convincing ones? The enforcers are the stick to their carrot.<br /> <br /> There. Nice, neat, <i>plausible</i> story about why kindred secretly <i>deserve </i>to rule mankind, because they couldn't stand to be alone forever. Maybe not... precisely logical, and perhaps I've left something out from slight forgetfulness about anthopology, or <i>slightly more</i> ignorance of anthropology, but it's near enough to sound convincing, which means it's at least good enough to fool vampires into feeling smug about themselves.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1289,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","user":"Hastur00","date":". 10-19-2015, 01:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T10:53:27-07:00","id":708995,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hastur00</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708985#post708985\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe not, but it makes a good story. Hell, it makes a good <i>legend</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, like I said, it's a fine thing for vampires to tell themselves, I already said I'd appreciate that as one of a dozen different origins vampires tell their impressionable neonates. But from a setting standpoint as a storyteller and player if I was looking at the Neolithic Vampire Dark Era and it described this, I'd be rolling my eyes and skipping over to the next section. It makes this NPC vampire a ridiculously forward-thinking and motivated vampire who ignores all of the drawbacks of being a vampire and manages to, in one fell sweep, create thing after thing that will echo throughout humanity for thousands of years, despite factual evidence suggesting they didn't even exist at that point.<br /> <br /> Essentially, it paints these ancient vampires as perfect and flawless, which Requiem really avoids doing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T11:53:40-07:00","id":709117,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708995#post708995\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yes, like I said, it's a fine thing for vampires to tell themselves, I already said I'd appreciate that as one of a dozen different origins vampires tell their impressionable neonates. But from a setting standpoint as a storyteller and player if I was looking at the Neolithic Vampire Dark Era and it described this, I'd be rolling my eyes and skipping over to the next section. It makes this NPC vampire a ridiculously forward-thinking and motivated vampire who ignores all of the drawbacks of being a vampire and manages to, in one fell sweep, create thing after thing that will echo throughout humanity for thousands of years, despite factual evidence suggesting they didn't even exist at that point.<br /> <br /> Essentially, it paints these ancient vampires as perfect and flawless, which Requiem really avoids doing.</div> </div> </div> </div>See though, Mages and Werewolves get the free pass of having had a previous utopia, a blueprint imprinted in their collective unconscious, of how things <i>were, could be, should be</i>, and they are able to operate unlike prehistoric men and women.<br /> <br /> Who says Vampires don't have that? Perhaps the pre-historic Vampires were Strix trying to <i>humanize</i> themselves, or perhaps the door through which they came into our world got narrow following certain cataclysmic events. These proto-Vampires had perfect memory of spirit assemblies, Strix parliaments, and organized societies in the Shadow, that they used to force humanity into an otherwise unnatural way of life and organization. In fact, the proto-Vampires could've pushed humanity to evolve both biologically and socially, so that their childer could more closely resemble themselves.<br /> <br /> That's just me, half-assing coming up with something to justify why if Vampire only gets a bullet point in the Prehistoric Era, it should be an interesting one that counts for something. Otherwise, the plot hook library has filled up and there's little reason for me to keep buying books.<br /> <br /> Edit: I'm not attacking the writers in any way, just thinking out loud that the game has not gone as far as other lines in nWoD 2nd edition, to let go of features from the 1st edition that limited its appeal to a certain group of players and STs. Everything I state in the discussion is so that I, personally, can overcome hurdles in my recruitment process for players. Feel free to disregard what I say, and I hope the writers understand that I have the utmost respect for their work (especially in 2nd edition).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":59,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","user":"stylanski","date":". 10-19-2015, 03:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T13:07:33-07:00","id":709192,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=709117#post709117\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">See though, Mages and Werewolves get the free pass of having had a previous utopia, a blueprint imprinted in their collective unconscious, of how things <i>were, could be, should be</i>, and they are able to operate unlike prehistoric men and women.</div> </div> </div> </div>You'll notice that their utopias were ruined, and didn't create the entire World of Darkness, though. Every aspect of human civilization didn't hinge upon werewolves or mages graciously gifting humanity with civilization, trade and law and horticulture and religion, which was the legend being suggested.<br /> <br /> Vampire the Masquerade was very much a game about the past affecting the present. Through the curse of Caine, the manipulations of the antediluvians and the progenitors of the clans, the Jyhad is a staple of the game, a war between the past and the present. Vampire the Requiem is about the now. It is about the Requiem, their period of unlife. Your character is a vampire, now they have to decide what they are going to do. The game is explicitly not about the past controlling the present and future.<br /> <br /> And that's part of the strix myth. Vampires are becoming something. There's a reason proto-vampires find themselves becoming more like 'standard' vampires, and why the origins of all the vampire clans are so widely divergent while, in modern day, seeming so similar. It's because they're slowly changing to be 'Vampire.' Everything is about the now, and the future, as an opposite of Masquerade, which was about the actions of the past and living in the now without a future. It's a neat inversion, and as noted above, if someone is a fan of Caine, with the toolbox setup there is nothing keeping you from integrating it into your Requiem setting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's just me, half-assing coming up with something to justify why if Vampire only gets a bullet point in the Prehistoric Era, it should be an interesting one that counts for something. Otherwise, the plot hook library has filled up and there's little reason for me to keep buying books. </div> </div> </div>From what I've heard vampires aren't 'just' strix-possessed corpses, but it does mention that they haven't formed clans yet. I like the company, but even I would strongly suggest not buying books solely for a sentence about a template. If you didn't back the Dark Eras book you could just ask a friend or someone on the forum to tell you what it says, it's supposed to be like one line, I believe. And if you do feel strongly about vampires wanting a past to their setting, when another Dark Eras or similar book pops up, get more people to vote for vampire. Its been eight months and I'm still shocked and disappointed about that.<br /> <br /> But even if we do get a book about the past, and I'm sure a Thousand Years of Night will at least delve into it a little, I'm hoping it's not about Johnny Vampireseed and how he created civilization.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 10-19-2015, 04:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T13:09:50-07:00","id":709194,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=709117#post709117\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's just me, half-assing coming up with something to justify why if Vampire only gets a bullet point in the Prehistoric Era, it should be an interesting one that counts for something. Otherwise, the plot hook library has filled up and there's little reason for me to keep buying books.<br /> <br /> Edit: I'm not attacking the writers in any way, just thinking out loud that the game has not gone as far as other lines in nWoD 2nd edition, to let go of features from the 1st edition that limited its appeal to a certain group of players and STs. Everything I state in the discussion is so that I, personally, can overcome hurdles in my recruitment process for players. Feel free to disregard what I say, and I hope the writers understand that I have the utmost respect for their work (especially in 2nd edition).</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that any bullet points should be interesting ones that count for something. If it doesn't account for a compelling plot hook, then why should it even be there?<br /> <br /> I'd argue that the ambiguous history makes Vampire more inclusive to a larger group of players. There is no canon origin for vampires, which means you can interpret their origins however you want in your game. I don't see how that's a flaw of earlier editions. It seems instead to be a reflection of the game's themes. Werewolf and Mage are about a fall from grace. They're predicated on the idea that these creatures used to be something great, who tumbled from a utopia due to their hubris and now subsist in a shadow of their former glory. Vampire doesn't have that as a canon factor because that's not what the game is about, but it's a perfectly valid bit of history that STs can graft onto their own setting.<br /> <br /> Out of curiosity, what are the hurdles you find yourself facing when trying to recruit for VtR?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8492,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","user":"dxanders","date":". 10-19-2015, 04:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T13:41:54-07:00","id":709231,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=709192#post709192\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm hoping it's not about Johnny Vampireseed and how he created civilization.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, come on, that's just ungenerous.<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708995#post708995\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Essentially, it paints these ancient vampires as perfect and flawless, which Requiem really avoids doing.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Oh, no, certainly, the idea of a society of noble vampire Forefathers breaking and taming and </span></span></span><i>befriending </i><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">humanity, fangs held high, is a ludicrous image, appropriate only for satire or propaganda. But one of the reasons I found it so compelling is that, strip out the manifest destiny and whi- </span></span></span><i>dead </i><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">man's burden angles of the story, and you're left with something that I think </span></span></span><i>could </i><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">actually flow naturally from &quot;vampires being vampires for ten thousand years&quot;. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">With respect, I don't think it's possible because vampires are especially smart or innovative- I think it's because they're </span></span></span><i>old, </i><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">and the longer-lived you are, the more you're forced to think long-term, and the more information you gather.</span></span></span><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"> A vampire who exists before written language or, Lord, before record-keeping, will have the advantage of witnessing (at least) several generations of change, biological and otherwise, to determine how their world works, how people work, how the animals and death and the sky </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">work, all from one perspective and set of memories. When local technological achievement can be summarized as &quot;grow small plants bigger to feed more people, because that's a thing that can be done&quot;, IMO it wouldn't take an outrageous length of time for at least one kindred to get the idea to stick on a local scale. Strip out the &quot;better for each other and for ourselves&quot; self-congratulatory bull of the legend of vampiric cooperation, and take a step back, and it doesn't look too different from isolated pockets of people gradually learning new technology, settling, then bouncing new ideas and technologies off each other until someone has the pull or need to put all the pieces together at once. I imagined it less as &quot;stupid humans, this is how cities and technology work&quot; and more a gradual, organic development due to human, kindred, and </span></span></span><i>individual </i><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">kindred interests dovetailing over the course of millennia.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">That said, I don't think that it's </span></span></span><i>true</i><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">, because of reasons you said.<br /> <br /> ...buuut I'm starting to think if it wouldn't be a terrible idea if it </span></span></span><i>was </i><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">true, because-</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=709117#post709117\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">See though, Mages and Werewolves get the free pass of having had a previous utopia, a blueprint imprinted in their collective unconscious, of how things <i>were, could be, should be</i>, and they are able to operate unlike prehistoric men and women.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">-because vampires are, in a way, the odd man out in the WoD. Except under rare circumstances, they have little or no ability to traverse or effect a spirit realm. They're, by and large, without the ability to effect or perceive cosmic-scale gameplay They are utterly, &quot;biologically&quot;, dependent on humans in a way that the other splats aren't. They don't even have a cohesive origin story. They're secular, in a respect that the others aren't.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">So, while I originally just intended this to be a thought experiment, it's starting to seem oddly fitting. Every other splat has some... knowledge about the greater universe, some special hyperspace playground, some <i>secret </i>about the world that's not just the fact that they exist. I personally find it kind of boss that<br /> maybe vampires can say they've </span></span></span><i>already made</i><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"> that kind of fundamental conceptual contribution, and moreover, it's one that the others always just took for granted.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=709192#post709192\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Everything is about the now, and the future, as an opposite of Masquerade, which was about the actions of the past and living in the now without a future. It's a neat inversion,</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">It </span></span></span><i>does </i><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">make </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">a pretty heavy-handed metaphor, yeah. And of course it doesn't fit what little we </span></span></span><i>do </i><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">know about the strix and the early world, so... </span></span></span><i>that's </i><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">freely acknowledged.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Call it one for the conspiracy theory vault, then</span></span></span><img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1289,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","user":"Hastur00","date":". 10-19-2015, 04:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T13:58:30-07:00","id":709240,"title":"","content":"It's somewhat ironic that vampire is one of the splats least anchored in the past*, while we've gotten the most historical information about it, through the Rome books and Ancient books. I think the Camarilla formed in Rome was considered by vampires at the time, in Rome, to be the height of vampire organization, while older vampires in the know would sort of roll their eyes thinking, themselves, about some great vampire community of the past that had fallen. Almost every chapter in the Ancients book describes some great feat of community once you go back far enough, with En Isiratuu ruling all of a nation for a tremendously long time as En, or Lord, of vampires. While both the Ancients books and clanbook Mekhet delve a bit into the Egyptian past and their strong influence there, where they even worked alongside a hunter conspiracy to defend Egypt from supernatural threats.<br /> <br /> But like human legends and human places, these things fall with time, the survivors scatter, specifics are lost, holes in memories open and fill with hopes or hatreds. The Lancea et Sanctum is called out for actually hiding or destroying historical evidence of vampire 'civilization' from long ago. It's all very interesting.<br /> <br /> I always said it would be nice to have a universal timeline book, similar to classic's Dark Ages, though not Dark Ages. And while before I thought Rome would be a good era to start, everyone seems very gung-ho for Neolithic Era splats.<br /> <br /> *: Alongside changeling and demon, which have practically no ties, and sin-eaters, which has little.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 10-19-2015, 05:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T14:10:11-07:00","id":709269,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=709240#post709240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's somewhat ironic that vampire is one of the splats least anchored in the past, while we've gotten the most historical information about it, through the Rome books and Ancient books. I think the Camarilla formed in Rome was considered by vampires at the time, in Rome, to be the height of vampire organization, while older vampires in the know would sort of roll their eyes thinking, themselves, about some great vampire community of the past that had fallen. Almost every chapter in the Ancients book describes some great feat of community once you go back far enough, with En Isiratuu ruling all of a nation for a tremendously long time as En, or Lord, of vampires. While both the Ancients books and clanbook Mekhet delve a bit into the Egyptian past and their strong influence there, where they even worked alongside a hunter conspiracy to defend Egypt from supernatural threats.<br /> <br /> But like human legends and human places, these things fall with time, the survivors scatter, specifics are lost, holes in memories open and fill with hopes or hatreds.<br /> <br /> I always said it would be nice to have a universal timeline book, similar to classic's Dark Ages, though not Dark Ages. And while before I thought Rome would be a good era to start, everyone seems very gung-ho for Neolithic Era splats.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd go with Rome or the Renaissance. The latter covers a rather expansive period of time and could cover everything from Elizabethan England to the Italian city-states to the Ottoman Empire. I think Renaissance Italy would be an even more compelling setting for Vampire than the Roman Empire.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T14:28:56-07:00","id":709305,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=708796#post708796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have more than enough books and pdfs that detail an extremely long and interesting part of or time in the lore <b>by a bullet point story hook</b> (that's my main issue and argument here) or a mix and twist of the 5 + 5. All it took for the Movement to rise was a flyer by some random French vampire, not the centuries of scientific research and intense, violent debates that the mortal Left actually experienced. Yes, that is different from the Anarchs of Masquerade, but it is sinfully simplistic.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's wrong. That was the moment of the formation of the Carthians as a Covenant, but that doesn't mean that it came from nowhere. In first edition (althugh that could have been decanonized), the Movement is the final version of the ideas of some Enlightened Kindred who had been practicing new forms of goverment for a hundred years or so.<br /> <br /> And it's not that the common vampire was absolutelly ignored until then: we had the Peregrine Collegia in Ancient Rome, and in Ancient Mysteries, there was an Anarch revolt in 17th century, during 30 years war. Corebook has a limited space of words, but there has been hints in other books telling us that Carthans dind' appear from nowhere and that they had precedents in time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>LAND OF THE DAMNED: SPAIN (Spanish): </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/3MHWYm\" target=\"_blank\">Land of the Damned: Spain</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/52b0TX\" target=\"_blank\">Kingdoms of Blood: Spain</a>; Cities of the Damned: <a href=\"https://goo.gl/XFMSSe\" target=\"_blank\">Barcelona</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/AXxdgq\" target=\"_blank\">Valencia</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/zkI5a1\" target=\"_blank\">Carthian Constitution</a> (1812), <a href=\"https://goo.gl/5H8M4s\" target=\"_blank\">Three Arrows Pact</a>: <br /> <br /> <b>OTHERS (Spanish): </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/36HngB\" target=\"_blank\">Demon: The Redemption</a>, <a href=\"https://goo.gl/6B9V8l\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Forgotten</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/247-uxas","name":"Uxas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar247_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T14:40:55-07:00","id":709311,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>dxanders</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=709194#post709194\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Out of curiosity, what are the hurdles you find yourself facing when trying to recruit for VtR?</div> </div> </div> </div>I had two in the same week. First, there was this old group of players I had initiated into VtR (people that do not even do pc gaming but had actually started reading books).<br /> We stopped playing for a year and a half, until we all moved back to our home city. We met and discussed about playing three very interesting chronicles I had prepared. But as it turns out, during our time apart, all but one of them had started VtM Dark Ages (set in Constantinople).<br /> <br /> So, when we discussed my three chronicles, they said they really liked the premise, but! After playing VtM, they wish I would also switch to that for the simple reason that it has an established role, players can sympathize with clans, their histories, they had picked sides on the Anarch Revolt, even though they haven't played it yet.<br /> <br /> Then there was this other group I met at a Requiem LARP, who at first seemed pretty excited, then one of them read up the Lasombra and the Path of Night, and ended up getting the group excited for VtM.<br /> <br /> It was one thing to argue with VtM veterans about running Requiem with me. It's a whole different level arguing with people whose nostalgia defaults to Requiem, and yet they still prefer VtM's lore.<br /> <br /> My problem is, I don't want to switch to VtM. I like the cleaner rules, I almost exclusively play and ST Requiem, and absolutely enjoy the freedom it allows me. But does it really have to go from metaplot extravaganza to city crawling with fangs? :-/","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":59,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","user":"stylanski","date":". 10-19-2015, 05:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T15:18:20-07:00","id":709327,"title":"","content":"Looking at some of the replies here, I feel like we've read different books.<br /> <br /> Any amount of lore is too Masquerade? Sure, before the Clan books. I feel like if you've paid attention to anything afterwards, you've seen the polar opposite in play. More than that, the lore introduced in the clan books and supplements like Ancient Mysteries and Ancient Bloodlines built a really fascinating history for Kindred that is a <i>Kindred </i>history that isn't so lazy as to suggest that vampires did things <i>for </i>humans.<br /> <br /> Sure, a vampire might <i>say </i>that, but if you look at how Kindred society has evolved, it's quite obvious that Requiem prefers that Kindred (as people) are shaped by history ,not the other way around. Sort of like how real history hasn't <i>REALLY </i>been made by individuals, but by very complex processes and factors that at some points, individuals have been in positions to exploit.<br /> <br /> We don't need vampires to encourage humans to agriculture so they can feed on them. What we need and what makes sense for Requiem is that people learned agriculture, and the Kindred <i>adapted </i>to it alongside them. In fact, if you look at 2e's core, it's more clear than ever that Kindred are <i>very </i>adaptable, and no doubt that presents a good justification as to why they can be so shaped by the history happening around them (see: the Carthian Movement as one example)<br /> <br /> But really, Requiem has no lore? Half of <i>Ancient Mysteries </i>was lore. Look at the 2e core. All that stuff about dead covenants? About the Strix at Alexandria, or a weaponized masquerade in Transylvania? Is that not lore? Does that not give you a sense of the world, that Kindred would call what happened in Transylvania a war with the Strix? That the rise and fall of organizations like the Gallow's Post, or the Children's Crusade don't clearly give an idea that lore happened here? The history is there if you care to look for it, and if <i>Requiem for Regina </i>is anything to go by, there's definitely clear cut ideas about what went on with that history.<br /> <br /> Are we really saying Requiem doesn't have lore? It's lore is unobtrusive, but it is <i>very much </i>present, and definitely distinct from Masquerade.<br /> <br /> I also feel like it's worth pointing out we're getting a book called <i>A Thousand Years of Night</i>. About playing elders, people who've lived through that history. Not unlike <i>Ancient Mysteries.</i> How much you want to bet we're gonna get some more meat in that book to go with.<br /> <br /> EDIT: And lastly, I see people complaining about the idea of Strix-possessed corpses and a time before the Clans, but I feel like it's worth pointing out that Neolithic Mage and Neolithic Werewolf seem to be in similar states of uncertainty in their form.<br /> <br /> The fact that the Orders are not fully developed in <i>To The Strongest </i>and that mages don't necessarily recognize all of their metaphysics the same way goes a <i>LONG </i>way towards giving the gameline a sense of history and development. <br /> <br /> I'm very fond of <i>Ancient Mysteries</i>, but when it presented us with the En and the other factions (obviously, they come after this neolithic dark era) they're arguably a lazy version of attempting to provide history, because they seem to show Vampires in the same way they are now. Actually doing something <i>DIFFERENT </i>really lends itself to providing vampires with History. We've heard it said that the Curse is alive and ever-evolving. We see it in Bloodlines, Clans like the Pijavica or the Jiangshi. It'd be great to also see it reflected in more amorphous proto-vampire beings. I'm just saying, if you want to give Vampire more history, you could do a lot worse then demonstrating how the Curse has developed from what it was in this time period.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":159,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","user":"Leetsepeak","date":". 10-19-2015, 06:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T17:46:09-07:00","id":709625,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=709327#post709327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">EDIT: And lastly, I see people complaining about the idea of Strix-possessed corpses and a time before the Clans, but I feel like it's worth pointing out that Neolithic Mage and Neolithic Werewolf seem to be in similar states of uncertainty in their form.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a good point. Since the era is pre-Sundering, it is before the creation of the Pure and Forsaken, and before the formation of the nine tribes.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T01:38:24-07:00","id":710162,"title":"","content":"This story is true.<br /> <br /> You go back far enough, all you find is blood and smoke. It's dark, hazy, and when you see any light at all, it's red. It feels like rising from an ocean of blood, taking the first tentative steps onto land, when the moon was full and pale, unclouded by smogs and blotted out by skyscrapers. We knew Thirst then, as we know it now. The further from the ocean of blood we crawled the thirstier for its warmth we became. The People were there, the Kine, their hearts beating with such enticing vitality. Other animals could slake our thirst, but not like the People. The sweetness of their blood eclipsed all others, a wine that raised us from ravenous beasts to refined hunters. As their civilization grew, so did ours. We were there, in the shadows, watching, hunting, choosing from among them to become like us. Perhaps that is what damned us so, or maybe we were always damned. We arose with our own demons, the dark ones, the Birds of Dis. As we haunted the Kine, the Strix haunted us. As we hunted the People, the Strix hunted the Kindred. In those early days we fought with them, for they would have had us tear down the People and give in to our predatory urges. They whispered with wicked voices, spoke to us as children, as pups learning the art of killing from their mother. We were born not to live among them but to devour everything the People made. Tear it apart, shatter it, plunge it into eternal night.<br /> <br /> Some of us gave in. The Strix took others by force, possessing their bodies and driving them to bloody madness. We gathered together with others of our kind and we fought the possessed ones, the mad howling revenants, and the disembodied spirits of smoke and blood. Dark times, indeed. Even we feared the night, but dared not gather too near to the fire. Those of us who in desperation butchered villages soon met their ends at the hands of the Wise. The Lupines were there even as we were, hunting their favored prey, heedless of our struggles just to survive. We slept in the earth to escape the sun's grasp. At night we crept out to find what mortals were too foolish or unlucky to be caught away from the fires and talismans of their villages. Sometimes the Strix were waiting then, reanimating those we had slain and sending them against us. The most desperate of our number sought help from the Wise, even beseeched the Lupines to help drive away the Strix. Most found disappointment, many Final Death.<br /> <br /> In the end, we had but one option to survive: adapt or die. Like the Kine, we found ourselves in a dark and dangerous world, full of predators outside the walls. Humanity drew strength from its community, in numbers and teamwork. So would we. The greatest of our kind gathered into flocks. Bloodlines spread. The greatest of these bloodlines became the clans we know today, but back then there were many more. Each its own species within an unholy genus, these &quot;proto-vampires&quot; banded together with a singular purpose: raising up the Kine, that the Kindred might live among them. We aided the People by night where they needed us, tirelessly tilling soil and guarding them from the predators that stalked them at night. From observing their ways we refined our own magic, and by feeding from them we grew stronger still. When the Strix came to set our villages to ruin, we had the People with their fire to help drive them away. As we grew stronger, our blood magic more powerful, we too came to fight back. We lured them into the bodies of the dead buried in unguarded graveyards, our thralls trapped them and put them to the torch. We adopted the talismans of the People and so used them against the Birds of Dis. In the larger settlements the Strix became a grave threat only in numbers, while the Kindred continued to grow with the People.<br /> <br /> We had a chance at last to become something more than savage revenants. With art and burgeoning civilization came opportunity to develop philosophies during the night, to learn crafts besides those of Disciplines and the art of killing. Some of us met to discuss those philosophies, or to form cults, or simply to socialize with others that shared our burdens. For a time we forgot what it meant to be the hunted, save for those of us foolish enough to provoke entire settlements to torch-wielding ire. We grew, and we prospered, and though few settlements could sustain many of us, we established hierarchies, traditions to help us survive. They would become the Traditions in time, but the world then was different, and we had different needs. If we were to be the Kindred, bound together by Night and Thirst, we needed some form of structure. Thus were our Traditions:<br /> <br /> <b>To the Shadows Cleave</b><br /> When the People knew of bloodthirsty monsters in their midst they would destroy us. They would turn their rites and their Wise against us, they would find us where we slept and expose us to the sunlight. The People knew the terrible world and its many dangers, and they accepted them. Spirits and other beasts from dark realms hunted them, terrorized villages as did the mortal beasts of the time. When we were hidden, a danger lurking in the night, we could survive, for the People did not wage crusades to wipe out the creatures of the night. Those of us who did walk openly among the People did so as godlike figures worshiped by entire village cults. We found that feeding in the shadows, or upon the monsters that would prey upon the People, kept us alive. Out of sight, out of mind, or at least away from the end of a burning brand.<br /> <br /> <b>Raise Only Your Own Dead</b><br /> In the Neolithic Era we had the power to Embrace even corpses. Many Kindred poisoned the waters of the People with Vitae, slowly influencing them. This meant that many mortals had tasted Vitae, however diluted. The Kindred discovered that they could then raise up these corpses as their own by feeding them Vitae. Cults formed around this, entire vampiric lineages born out of rituals of unhallowed rebirth. Not all corpses stayed dead, or rose at the whim of vampires playing god. Revenants clawed their way from the grave, murdering their living kin before dying to flame or fang. Some foolish vampires were themselves destroyed when a revenant killed the entire village, leaving the Kindred to die in the wastes or fall to one another's fangs. Eventually among our more civilized kind grew an understanding: only Embrace those who live or die directly at your own fangs. Poison not the waters or risk the undead rising and shattering lives in berserk rage.<br /> <br /> <b>Know Humanity</b><br /> We have an old saying: &quot;Even the heartless can be staked.&quot; In the Neolithic it was perhaps easier than in modern nights to forsake Humanity. Society existed as far as the pastures and crops. Law vanished as did the unwary into the wilds. Beyond the River, perhaps the Mountain loomed in the distance, and the world beyond that remained a mystery. The Kindred did not have this byzantine structure of modern societal laws to rely upon for guidance. If a vampire desired a maiden for lust or for blood, she could simply take that maiden. If a fool offended her, she could simply tear out his tongue to silence him. Those who formed cults that worshiped them as deathly gods lost sight of who they once were, perhaps one of those very villagers that now sacrificed their own to sate the vampire. Piece by piece we could fall into bestial madness, when we had worked so hard to rise above that. We needed to cleave to Humanity so that the Beast did not rule. It was said in those times that when the Beast won, it cast off dead flesh and went unshackled, becoming a creature of smoke and evil. To remain in control we had to retain a sense of refinement. We were better predators because we were the Kindred, not the mindless killers.<br /> <br /> You might notice that there was no Tradition against Amaranth. Blasphemy, some say. Lunacy. It was simply assumed, a base truth from which all other Traditions sprang. I say differently. For we were predators then as we are tonight. While often predators avoid direct battle with one another, competition is a part of the cycle of life and undeath. In conflict we grow. Our desperation to escape predation by the Strix made us stronger, more advanced. A thousand different &quot;clans,&quot; or what we might call such, existed then, each seeking to further its line. Not all of them could survive, just as not all species survive in the wild. We killed one another. We <i>ate </i>one another, and we changed because of it. Bloodlines merged and developed new traits, while others vanished into extinction. The strongest persevered. Some of the clans hit their peak, becoming diluted, their powers of the blood thinned by successive generations. Time only strengthened others. But in the end, the greatest of the clans survived into the modern night. Don't be fooled: many more are still out there, remnants of the Neolithic slumbering in earthen graves or waiting to reemerge in modern bloodlines.<br /> <br /> Being a vampire in the Neolithic meant many things. Above all it meant trial against great difficulties. Alcohol, drugs, raves with pounding music and stupefied inhibitions, none of these things existed. Popular culture painting the Kindred as sexy and mysterious was millennia from its emergence. The People were not packed in like sardines in a tin, waiting to feel the pleasure of the Kiss. We fought to survive, to protect our mortal families, to establish laws and societies in a world that would not accommodate us. We were rugged, and we were potent. The Kindred of the Neolithic had no sacrosanct feeding grounds, no blood dolls. They went out and hunted, fed upon one another, stalked the beasts and the spirits that wandered the dangerous wilds beyond the River. We survived. In time, we even thrived. Though the youth (and all too many of our elders) today forget it, once we were the prey as well as the predator.<br /> <br /> Now, the Kindred are apex predators, but only thanks to the legacy of our Neolithic forebears.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Creating a Neolithic Kindred Character</b></span><br /> When creating a Neolithic Kindred character, use the following changes to the vampire template presented in the <b>Vampire: The Requiem Second Edition </b>rulebook.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Clans: </b>The clans as recognized by modern Kindred do not yet exist, or at least dominate vampire-kind. Many clans exist, including those whose evolution will transform them into the modern day clans. Instead of choosing a clan in the book, players of Neolithic vampires define their own bloodlines that have given rise to the character. Because the vampires of the Neolithic were more hardy by necessity, they gain one dot in each of three Attributes: one Power, one Finesse, and one Resistance. The player may choose all the same category, or spread them among the Physical, Mental, or Social Attributes.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Disciplines:</b> An extension of defining one's own clan includes choosing the clan Disciplines. Each vampire may choose any three Disciplines as her in-clan Disciplines, with one limitation: one of the Disciplines <i>must </i>be one of the physical Disciplines: Celerity, Resilience, or Vigor. The Coils of the Dragon and Theban Sorcery do not exist, and so vampire characters cannot take these powers.Cr\u00faac exists but it is not widely known (only vampires with Occult 3+ may learn it), and its usage is tied to the Strix. Too many Cr\u00faac rituals often tend to bring the Birds of Dis to an area, with disastrous results.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Humanity:</b> Kindred begin with Humanity 5. The struggles to survive amid a backdrop lacking in the more advanced society of modern eras means that a vampire faces greater challenges in reclaiming her Humanity.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Feeding Restrictions:</b> Vampires of this era can feed on spirits and werewolves, though both prospects bring with them the risk of Final Death. Spirits are common in the mortal world, crossing through the Border Marches to live among those of flesh and blood. The People accept this as a fact of life, and so do the Kindred. Essentially, all vampires begin with two free dots in the Unnatural Affinity Merit, allowing them to feed from spirits and werewolves. Pangaea is a realm in which few vampires survive for any length of time, and so developing further Unnatural Affinities for Pangaeans is unlikely but not impossible. Feeding upon these gods of Pangaea is likely to result in something arcane and strange happening to the vampire.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Merits:</b> The following Merits are not available to Neolithic vampires: Status (Clan) or Status (Covenant), and any Merits that use them as prerequisites. The Altar Merit is an exception, which does not require Circle of the Crone Status, instead requiring Occult 3 (as does Cr\u00faac). The use of the Altar Merit is sure to draw the attention of the Strix sooner or later. Dream Visions is available to any vampire with Auspex as a clan Discipline. Kiss of the Succubus likewise becomes available to any vampire with Majesty as a clan Discipline, Pack Alpha becomes available through Animalism, and Unsettling Gaze through Nightmare. City Status is replaced by &quot;Village Status,&quot; functioning largely the same, even if it doesn't cover quite so populous an area. Carthian Law Merits and Invictus Oaths are unavailable.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Clan Banes:</b> Players should work with Storytellers to define a suitable &quot;clan bane&quot; for their character. Any character may use the banes listed in the book as inspiration or as their personal bane. Because of their starting Humanity, all vampire characters spend their unlives with their clan bane active. Storytellers may wish to allow vampires who reach Humanity 10, rare as it is, to shed their clan banes and only regain them if they drop to Humanity 6 or lower again during their unlives.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Freelancer </b>\u2022 Available for work!","edited":{"userid":109,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield","user":"Claire Redfield","date":". 10-20-2015, 04:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":25}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield","name":"Claire Redfield","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar109_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T04:38:36-07:00","id":710367,"title":"","content":"Hmm, did any kind of megafauna still persist into the Neolithic?<br /> <br /> I think at least one way to make vampires more manageable in such games is to say that these early version have an easier time feeding on animals, while holding to feeding on animals being perilous.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T05:09:36-07:00","id":710386,"title":"","content":"It sounds like most of them died off during the Pleistocene era, before the Neolithic.<br /> <br /> But you had the birth of agriculture and settlements with a few thousand people. Not exactly cities, there were animals, dogs, cattle, pig, sheep. Apparently horses didn't come into the mix to be ridden for a while afterward, though it might still be in the Neolithic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Claire Redfield</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=710162#post710162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You might notice that there was no Tradition against Amaranth. Blasphemy, some say. Lunacy. It was simply assumed, a base truth from which all other Traditions sprang. I say differently.</div> </div> </div> </div>I always liked the Edimmu as the reason for the tradition against Amaranth.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 10-20-2015, 08:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T05:33:00-07:00","id":710421,"title":"","content":"Mammoths were still around when the Pyramids were being built. Other than that, I'm not sure.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T08:16:01-07:00","id":710615,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=710386#post710386\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It sounds like most of them died off during the Pleistocene era, before the Neolithic.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Yeah, the last ice age wasn't kind. They fared even worse in Australia.</span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T09:44:32-07:00","id":710789,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Claire Redfield</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=710162#post710162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Piece of awesomeness</div> </div> </div> </div>Is that the Prehistoric Dark Era Vampire page? If this was written to shut me up on every single point I was complaining about, it worked.<br /> Awesome work!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T10:08:00-07:00","id":710847,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=710789#post710789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is that the Prehistoric Dark Era Vampire page? If this was written to shut me up on every single point I was complaining about, it worked.<br /> Awesome work!</div> </div> </div> </div> Nope - it's a really cool piece of writing, but DaveB wrote the sidebar for Other Supernaturals In The Neolithic (tm) and he had less wordcount for the entire thing, all lines, than Claire used just for Vampire there <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> Were I to have had the extra 5k words to include Requiem in the chapter, I think I'd probably tie Kindred into the [REDACTED] that's going on with the Underworld at the time...<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>- Chris Allen, Aberrant Line Developer, Freelance Writer<br /> <br /> \u200bLike my work? Feel like helping me stay supplied with tea? Check out my <b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/acrozatarim\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1757-acrozatarim","name":"Acrozatarim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T12:54:27-07:00","id":711134,"title":"","content":"As Chris said, it was just something I opted to write for y'all. I felt a bit inspired last night and so I came up with that to at least give the folks wanting to play vampires in the Neolithic a little something to work with. Tying in the Kindred to the Underworld could be a lot of fun.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Freelancer </b>\u2022 Available for work!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield","name":"Claire Redfield","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar109_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T13:42:41-07:00","id":711199,"title":"","content":"Awesome stuff, Claire. I really appreciate the fluff accompanying the stat ideas. I think the only obvious question I have is that your Humanity section might want at least a sentence about Touchstones. Are they detached as a consequence of your starting Humanity? That would incidentally make a lot of sense with regards to my thought that Humanity is just way harder to maintain, and that you are probably more likely to run into draugr than functioning Kindred.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Acrozatarim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=710847#post710847\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <br /> Nope - it's a really cool piece of writing, but DaveB wrote the sidebar for Other Supernaturals In The Neolithic (tm) and he had less wordcount for the entire thing, all lines, than Claire used just for Vampire there <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> Were I to have had the extra 5k words to include Requiem in the chapter, I think I'd probably tie Kindred into the [REDACTED] that's going on with the Underworld at the time...</div> </div> </div> </div>I can't wait to see it! Not asking for spoile ra obviously since we wouldn't get them anyway, but does what Claire wrote up dovetail with Dave's sentence for the Kindred at all? Or failing that, is there anything contradictory? Just curious, it'd be awesome if we could supplement it with Claire's work.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T14:14:45-07:00","id":711223,"title":"","content":"Loved Claire write-up. If any player insisted to play as a vampire, I would use it.<br /> <br /> However, I hope there isn't vampires, yet. Or they are just being born, only as complete monsters (probably Stryx) and evolve into kindred as humanity develop. I would really love to see that &quot;thing&quot; from the Mekhet Clanbook again.<br /> <br /> But I also hope that Viking and Roman Werewolves are different from the norm, a bit stronger or bestial. Taking the uratha from Bau and something like their &quot;blood&quot; is getting weaker through the ages, to what we have these days. I completely expect Neolithic Werewolves to be very different, but I think they already confirmed this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/196-max-deltree","name":"Max Deltree","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar196_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"178"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T17:23:48-07:00","id":711469,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Claire Redfield</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=711134#post711134\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As Chris said, it was just something I opted to write for y'all. I felt a bit inspired last night and so I came up with that to at least give the folks wanting to play vampires in the Neolithic a little something to work with. Tying in the Kindred to the Underworld could be a lot of fun.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your writing here is now canon in my brain.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Revlid wrote:<br /> Yes, hollowing out your humanity to become an utterly utilitarian asura is the exact suggestion I would expect from you, Aiden.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/168-aiden","name":"Aiden","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T18:35:48-07:00","id":711545,"title":"","content":"Well, thank you all. I'm glad you like it! If nothing else, it's at least a bit of a basis to go on for Neolithic vampires.<br /> <br /> I'm inclined to be a little merciful and say that you don't begin with detached Touchstones, but I'd have to think on it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Freelancer </b>\u2022 Available for work!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield","name":"Claire Redfield","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar109_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T19:39:21-07:00","id":711646,"title":"","content":"Oh no, make them acquire Touchstones in-game unless they shell out extra for it. Humans are so hard to find in this time period ...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Revlid wrote:<br /> Yes, hollowing out your humanity to become an utterly utilitarian asura is the exact suggestion I would expect from you, Aiden.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/168-aiden","name":"Aiden","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T22:00:00-07:00","id":711763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Claire Redfield</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=711134#post711134\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As Chris said, it was just something I opted to write for y'all. I felt a bit inspired last night and so I came up with that to at least give the folks wanting to play vampires in the Neolithic a little something to work with. Tying in the Kindred to the Underworld could be a lot of fun.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for that, it was an awesome nice write-up! In fact, I feel inclined to ask. Any chance we might get either a small pdf or blog post? I don't know about the others, but I would gladly pay to get something like that, plus a few proto-clans and disciplines.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T07:21:01-07:00","id":712369,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Retias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic#post705402\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've been pondering about vampires back in that age, with far smaller population numbers to draw on. A single village would likely only house one or two vampires, and if another tried moving in, it'd likely be very brutal.<br /> <br /> So vampires would face themselves as threats, and have to be extremely careful in their feeding to not draw suspicion. Without the heavy politics and scheming of modern day nights though, what sort of challenges would a vampire face, long term, beyond those already listed?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'd expect most vampires at that time to be trapped in low blood potency. Fewer humans means a greater need to be able to feed on animals, so most vampires would lapse into torpor around the 100 year mark. Shelter would be an issue. Most humans would live as nomadic hunter gatherers, so most vampires would either need natural shelter (i.e. caves) for the day, or need Protean to survive. Rapid humanity decline would be an issue, as fewer humans means more isolation. <br /> <br /> I'd also expect vampires to struggle with identity issues. Thier condition would exile most of them from their human tribes, and without the covenants to give them guidance, they would struggle with coming to understand what they had become. It would be interesting to see how a vampire with no pre-existing conception of vampirism adapts to their new state. Would he self-conceptualize as a &quot;spirit being&quot;, or believe he had been cursed by a higher power? <br /> <br /> The competition for access to human settlements and tribes would mean that dealing with revenants would be a substantial challenge.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6617-vyrkolash","name":"Vyrkolash","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T07:28:19-07:00","id":712378,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=711199#post711199\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can't wait to see it! Not asking for spoile ra obviously since we wouldn't get them anyway, but does what Claire wrote up dovetail with Dave's sentence for the Kindred at all? Or failing that, is there anything contradictory? Just curious, it'd be awesome if we could supplement it with Claire's work.</div> </div> </div> </div> I don't think there's anything <i>directly </i>contradictory, no, but again we had very little word count to spend on vampires etc in the first place.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Max Deltree</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=711223#post711223\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But I also hope that Viking and Roman Werewolves are different from the norm, a bit stronger or bestial. Taking the uratha from Bau and something like their &quot;blood&quot; is getting weaker through the ages, to what we have these days. I completely expect Neolithic Werewolves to be very different, but I think they already confirmed this.</div> </div> </div> </div> Neolithic werewolves are different, but not necessarily in the ways that people may be expecting based off the 1e Bau setting. Just as interesting are the ways that Neolithic werewolves are the <i>same </i>as modern Uratha...<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>- Chris Allen, Aberrant Line Developer, Freelance Writer<br /> <br /> \u200bLike my work? Feel like helping me stay supplied with tea? Check out my <b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/acrozatarim\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1757-acrozatarim","name":"Acrozatarim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T07:30:55-07:00","id":712380,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vyrkolash</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=712369#post712369\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The competition for access to human settlements and tribes would mean that dealing with revenants would be a substantial challenge.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even competition with other vampires would be a bigger deal. There were settlements, but vampires are already dealing with a small populace that has control of fire and a lack of mobility issues. Just having one extra vampire can turn a nice deal into an untenable situation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T10:13:09-07:00","id":712625,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=712380#post712380\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even competition with other vampires would be a bigger deal. There were settlements, but vampires are already dealing with a small populace that has control of fire and a lack of mobility issues. Just having one extra vampire can turn a nice deal into an untenable situation.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, but what makes the revenant issue so pernicious is that they can arise randomly. Other vampires would be relatively few and far between, but what do you do when your most loyal ghoul dies in battle, only to crawl out of his grave and start causing trouble.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6617-vyrkolash","name":"Vyrkolash","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T10:24:05-07:00","id":712636,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vyrkolash</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=712369#post712369\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd also expect vampires to struggle with identity issues. Thier condition would exile most of them from their human tribes, and without the covenants to give them guidance, they would struggle with coming to understand what they had become. It would be interesting to see how a vampire with no pre-existing conception of vampirism adapts to their new state.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why <i>don't </i>vampires like fire? Because they got sick of watching everybody else but them sit around it for three thousand years!<br /> <br /> Aaand I made myself sad.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T11:22:54-07:00","id":712775,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vyrkolash</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=712625#post712625\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> True, but what makes the revenant issue so pernicious is that they can arise randomly. Other vampires would be relatively few and far between, but what do you do when your most loyal ghoul dies in battle, only to crawl out of his grave and start causing trouble.</div> </div> </div> </div>Either suck him dry and bury his head apart from his body, or have him do the same to you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://eoinsstuff.shoutwiki.com/wiki/Genius:_the_Transgression_(2nd_Edition)\" target=\"_blank\">Genius: the Transgression 2E is a thing that's being worked on.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4488-malonkey1","name":"malonkey1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4488_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T11:52:05-07:00","id":712802,"title":"","content":"The crossover sidebars in Dark Eras are 500 words in each setting. Now, we don't have to cover Werewolves or Spirits (or Claimed) in that total for the Neolithic, thanks to the whole era being a mage/werewolf crossover, but it's still not an awful lot of room.<br /> <br /> We do talk about Strix as breath-stealing shadow-Owls, and we do (separately) talk about corpses that get up thirsting for blood. They might be Strix victims, they might be revenants. Whether any full Kindred exist yet is an exercise for the reader/Storyteller.<br /> <br /> In the Alexandrian setting, the next most ancient one in Dark Eras which I *also* wrote the crossover section for, I describe a whole bunch of (real-world) blood-drinking monster mythologies, and it mentions the fact that they don't yet consider themselves kindred to one another. Lamia and Pisacha are given as much (or as little, depending on your pov) room as Daeva and Strix.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Dave Brookshaw</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw","name":"Dave Brookshaw","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2081"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T11:58:40-07:00","id":712807,"title":"","content":"The predominant bias in the Neolithic chapter is that the people of the time, who are very, painfully familiar with spirits thanks to the Sundering not yet having happened, interpret a lot of monsters as alien entities inhabiting bodies - there's very little difference, to them, between a Sin-Eater and a Vampire. Both are the result of something taking an improperly-buried corpse as a meat-suit.<br /> <br /> The Alexandrian chapter, on the other hand, views the various supernatural beings as their own societies and families, and downplays the &quot;used to be a human&quot; part of their nature. <br /> <br /> So, a Vinca shaman would say a vampire is a foreign spirit inhabiting a corpse. A Persian magus would say a vampire is a member of a hidden race of similar monsters.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Dave Brookshaw</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw","name":"Dave Brookshaw","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2081"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T12:28:24-07:00","id":712856,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>malonkey1</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=712775#post712775\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Either suck him dry and bury his head apart from his body, or have him do the same to you.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except we're talking about close-knit tribal family groups. That's not just Billy the ghoul who no one will miss, it's your herd's &quot;Uncle Billy&quot;. The small scale of human social groups during this time makes them more intimate, and makes maintaining good relationships with humans a survival issue.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6617-vyrkolash","name":"Vyrkolash","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T12:36:22-07:00","id":712866,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vyrkolash</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=712856#post712856\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Except we're talking about close-knit tribal family groups. That's not just Billy the ghoul who no one will miss, it's your herd's &quot;Uncle Billy&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Based on what Dave just said, I'd think he'd be less &quot;Uncle Billy&quot; and more &quot;The thing that lives in Uncle Billy's body that doesn't age, has magic powers, and loves that corpse that walks at night after drinking its blood.&quot;<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-26T03:42:51-07:00","id":728270,"title":"","content":"It might be worth noting that, although we don't (can't?) know much about vampire &quot;prehistory,&quot; we <i>do</i> know that vampires of all clans can live alongside stone-age groups, even relatively sparse hunter-gatherer populations. If you look through the setting material, it becomes apparent that there were vampires all over North America before the arrival of Europeans, including in many places that never adopted agriculture before colonization. The Sta-Au are one example, as are the vampires encountered by Cardinal Inglorious when he traveled to what's now San Francisco, and one of the stories from the Nosferatu clanbook involves a group of Native American Nosferatu being unearthed in the San Fernando Valley. Shadows of Mexico details the kindred of the stone tool-using civilizations of Mesoamerica, and also suggests in passing that there were Gangrel among the hunter-gatherers of the North American deserts and Nosferatu in the ancient Pacific Northwest.<br /> <br /> These vampires would have faced a lot of challenges (not the least of which is that people in these places didn't generally build a lot of structures that reliably kept out the light of day), but apparently they managed. Vampires in Neolithic or even Mesolithic Eurasia would be no less plausible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3209,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","user":"CatDoom","date":". 10-26-2015, 06:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-26T18:56:09-07:00","id":730697,"title":"","content":"If Isiratuu is even half as old as claimed by the En than he would still have been ancient by the time of the Neolithic Dark Era. That could be something interesting to explore.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/477-xenorath","name":"Xenorath","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"169"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-26T20:30:11-07:00","id":730936,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xenorath</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=730697#post730697\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If Isiratuu is even half as old as claimed by the En than he would still have been ancient by the time of the Neolithic Dark Era. That could be something interesting to explore.</div> </div> </div> </div>Kindred tend to lie, but I've got a feeling it isn't Insiratuu that's that old, but the Alu.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":159,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","user":"Leetsepeak","date":". 10-27-2015, 06:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-27T13:35:46-07:00","id":732983,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=730936#post730936\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Kindred tend to lie, but I've got a feeling it isn't Insiratuu that's that old, but the En.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is always a possibility that the Daeva are descended from a human who was somehow empowered by an Alu. It's extremely likely the Mekhet weren't created by the Strix and combined with the existence of all those other non-Kindred vampires, the Strix are almost certainly not the progenitors of all vampires.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/477-xenorath","name":"Xenorath","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"169"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-27T15:03:48-07:00","id":733164,"title":"","content":"Yeah, it could be one cool origin for the Daeva, for sure. It just struck me since their bloodline weakness did mention the whole Alu taking over thing. Given what Insiratuu did, it's very likely that it wasn't REALLY Insiratuu at all during his rule.<br /> <br /> In my game I had the idea that the Alu might be different shadow beings originating from that same non-place the Strix seem to have come from.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-28T00:37:26-07:00","id":734061,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xenorath</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=730697#post730697\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If Isiratuu is even half as old as claimed by the En than he would still have been ancient by the time of the Neolithic Dark Era. That could be something interesting to explore.</div> </div> </div> </div>Funny, I think the last time I read the Black Streets of Babylon section of <i>Ancient Mysteries</i> I must have read &quot;twelve thousand&quot; as &quot;twelve hundred&quot; or something. That is a pretty crazy long time to rule &quot;Babylonia,&quot; especially since there definitely wasn't a Babylon in the 12th millennium BCE. That said, it might be consistent of ancient Mesopotamian historiography, since some of those king lists assign really really long reigns to early kings.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-29T18:29:07-07:00","id":855408,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Claire Redfield</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=710162#post710162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This story is true.<br /> <br /> You go back far enough, all you find is blood and smoke. It's dark, hazy, and when you see any light at all, it's red. It feels like rising from an ocean of blood, taking the first tentative steps onto land, when the moon was full and pale, unclouded by smogs and blotted out by skyscrapers. We knew Thirst then, as we know it now. The further from the ocean of blood we crawled the thirstier for its warmth we became. The People were there, the Kine, their hearts beating with such enticing vitality. Other animals could slake our thirst, but not like the People. The sweetness of their blood eclipsed all others, a wine that raised us from ravenous beasts to refined hunters. As their civilization grew, so did ours. We were there, in the shadows, watching, hunting, choosing from among them to become like us. Perhaps that is what damned us so, or maybe we were always damned. We arose with our own demons, the dark ones, the Birds of Dis. As we haunted the Kine, the Strix haunted us. As we hunted the People, the Strix hunted the Kindred. In those early days we fought with them, for they would have had us tear down the People and give in to our predatory urges. They whispered with wicked voices, spoke to us as children, as pups learning the art of killing from their mother. We were born not to live among them but to devour everything the People made. Tear it apart, shatter it, plunge it into eternal night.<br /> <br /> Some of us gave in. The Strix took others by force, possessing their bodies and driving them to bloody madness. We gathered together with others of our kind and we fought the possessed ones, the mad howling revenants, and the disembodied spirits of smoke and blood. Dark times, indeed. Even we feared the night, but dared not gather too near to the fire. Those of us who in desperation butchered villages soon met their ends at the hands of the Wise. The Lupines were there even as we were, hunting their favored prey, heedless of our struggles just to survive. We slept in the earth to escape the sun's grasp. At night we crept out to find what mortals were too foolish or unlucky to be caught away from the fires and talismans of their villages. Sometimes the Strix were waiting then, reanimating those we had slain and sending them against us. The most desperate of our number sought help from the Wise, even beseeched the Lupines to help drive away the Strix. Most found disappointment, many Final Death.<br /> <br /> In the end, we had but one option to survive: adapt or die. Like the Kine, we found ourselves in a dark and dangerous world, full of predators outside the walls. Humanity drew strength from its community, in numbers and teamwork. So would we. The greatest of our kind gathered into flocks. Bloodlines spread. The greatest of these bloodlines became the clans we know today, but back then there were many more. Each its own species within an unholy genus, these &quot;proto-vampires&quot; banded together with a singular purpose: raising up the Kine, that the Kindred might live among them. We aided the People by night where they needed us, tirelessly tilling soil and guarding them from the predators that stalked them at night. From observing their ways we refined our own magic, and by feeding from them we grew stronger still. When the Strix came to set our villages to ruin, we had the People with their fire to help drive them away. As we grew stronger, our blood magic more powerful, we too came to fight back. We lured them into the bodies of the dead buried in unguarded graveyards, our thralls trapped them and put them to the torch. We adopted the talismans of the People and so used them against the Birds of Dis. In the larger settlements the Strix became a grave threat only in numbers, while the Kindred continued to grow with the People.<br /> <br /> We had a chance at last to become something more than savage revenants. With art and burgeoning civilization came opportunity to develop philosophies during the night, to learn crafts besides those of Disciplines and the art of killing. Some of us met to discuss those philosophies, or to form cults, or simply to socialize with others that shared our burdens. For a time we forgot what it meant to be the hunted, save for those of us foolish enough to provoke entire settlements to torch-wielding ire. We grew, and we prospered, and though few settlements could sustain many of us, we established hierarchies, traditions to help us survive. They would become the Traditions in time, but the world then was different, and we had different needs. If we were to be the Kindred, bound together by Night and Thirst, we needed some form of structure. Thus were our Traditions:<br /> <br /> <b>To the Shadows Cleave</b><br /> When the People knew of bloodthirsty monsters in their midst they would destroy us. They would turn their rites and their Wise against us, they would find us where we slept and expose us to the sunlight. The People knew the terrible world and its many dangers, and they accepted them. Spirits and other beasts from dark realms hunted them, terrorized villages as did the mortal beasts of the time. When we were hidden, a danger lurking in the night, we could survive, for the People did not wage crusades to wipe out the creatures of the night. Those of us who did walk openly among the People did so as godlike figures worshiped by entire village cults. We found that feeding in the shadows, or upon the monsters that would prey upon the People, kept us alive. Out of sight, out of mind, or at least away from the end of a burning brand.<br /> <br /> <b>Raise Only Your Own Dead</b><br /> In the Neolithic Era we had the power to Embrace even corpses. Many Kindred poisoned the waters of the People with Vitae, slowly influencing them. This meant that many mortals had tasted Vitae, however diluted. The Kindred discovered that they could then raise up these corpses as their own by feeding them Vitae. Cults formed around this, entire vampiric lineages born out of rituals of unhallowed rebirth. Not all corpses stayed dead, or rose at the whim of vampires playing god. Revenants clawed their way from the grave, murdering their living kin before dying to flame or fang. Some foolish vampires were themselves destroyed when a revenant killed the entire village, leaving the Kindred to die in the wastes or fall to one another's fangs. Eventually among our more civilized kind grew an understanding: only Embrace those who live or die directly at your own fangs. Poison not the waters or risk the undead rising and shattering lives in berserk rage.<br /> <br /> <b>Know Humanity</b><br /> We have an old saying: &quot;Even the heartless can be staked.&quot; In the Neolithic it was perhaps easier than in modern nights to forsake Humanity. Society existed as far as the pastures and crops. Law vanished as did the unwary into the wilds. Beyond the River, perhaps the Mountain loomed in the distance, and the world beyond that remained a mystery. The Kindred did not have this byzantine structure of modern societal laws to rely upon for guidance. If a vampire desired a maiden for lust or for blood, she could simply take that maiden. If a fool offended her, she could simply tear out his tongue to silence him. Those who formed cults that worshiped them as deathly gods lost sight of who they once were, perhaps one of those very villagers that now sacrificed their own to sate the vampire. Piece by piece we could fall into bestial madness, when we had worked so hard to rise above that. We needed to cleave to Humanity so that the Beast did not rule. It was said in those times that when the Beast won, it cast off dead flesh and went unshackled, becoming a creature of smoke and evil. To remain in control we had to retain a sense of refinement. We were better predators because we were the Kindred, not the mindless killers.<br /> <br /> You might notice that there was no Tradition against Amaranth. Blasphemy, some say. Lunacy. It was simply assumed, a base truth from which all other Traditions sprang. I say differently. For we were predators then as we are tonight. While often predators avoid direct battle with one another, competition is a part of the cycle of life and undeath. In conflict we grow. Our desperation to escape predation by the Strix made us stronger, more advanced. A thousand different &quot;clans,&quot; or what we might call such, existed then, each seeking to further its line. Not all of them could survive, just as not all species survive in the wild. We killed one another. We <i>ate </i>one another, and we changed because of it. Bloodlines merged and developed new traits, while others vanished into extinction. The strongest persevered. Some of the clans hit their peak, becoming diluted, their powers of the blood thinned by successive generations. Time only strengthened others. But in the end, the greatest of the clans survived into the modern night. Don't be fooled: many more are still out there, remnants of the Neolithic slumbering in earthen graves or waiting to reemerge in modern bloodlines.<br /> <br /> Being a vampire in the Neolithic meant many things. Above all it meant trial against great difficulties. Alcohol, drugs, raves with pounding music and stupefied inhibitions, none of these things existed. Popular culture painting the Kindred as sexy and mysterious was millennia from its emergence. The People were not packed in like sardines in a tin, waiting to feel the pleasure of the Kiss. We fought to survive, to protect our mortal families, to establish laws and societies in a world that would not accommodate us. We were rugged, and we were potent. The Kindred of the Neolithic had no sacrosanct feeding grounds, no blood dolls. They went out and hunted, fed upon one another, stalked the beasts and the spirits that wandered the dangerous wilds beyond the River. We survived. In time, we even thrived. Though the youth (and all too many of our elders) today forget it, once we were the prey as well as the predator.<br /> <br /> Now, the Kindred are apex predators, but only thanks to the legacy of our Neolithic forebears.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Creating a Neolithic Kindred Character</b></span><br /> When creating a Neolithic Kindred character, use the following changes to the vampire template presented in the <b>Vampire: The Requiem Second Edition </b>rulebook.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Clans: </b>The clans as recognized by modern Kindred do not yet exist, or at least dominate vampire-kind. Many clans exist, including those whose evolution will transform them into the modern day clans. Instead of choosing a clan in the book, players of Neolithic vampires define their own bloodlines that have given rise to the character. Because the vampires of the Neolithic were more hardy by necessity, they gain one dot in each of three Attributes: one Power, one Finesse, and one Resistance. The player may choose all the same category, or spread them among the Physical, Mental, or Social Attributes.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Disciplines:</b> An extension of defining one's own clan includes choosing the clan Disciplines. Each vampire may choose any three Disciplines as her in-clan Disciplines, with one limitation: one of the Disciplines <i>must </i>be one of the physical Disciplines: Celerity, Resilience, or Vigor. The Coils of the Dragon and Theban Sorcery do not exist, and so vampire characters cannot take these powers.Cr\u00faac exists but it is not widely known (only vampires with Occult 3+ may learn it), and its usage is tied to the Strix. Too many Cr\u00faac rituals often tend to bring the Birds of Dis to an area, with disastrous results.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Humanity:</b> Kindred begin with Humanity 5. The struggles to survive amid a backdrop lacking in the more advanced society of modern eras means that a vampire faces greater challenges in reclaiming her Humanity.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Feeding Restrictions:</b> Vampires of this era can feed on spirits and werewolves, though both prospects bring with them the risk of Final Death. Spirits are common in the mortal world, crossing through the Border Marches to live among those of flesh and blood. The People accept this as a fact of life, and so do the Kindred. Essentially, all vampires begin with two free dots in the Unnatural Affinity Merit, allowing them to feed from spirits and werewolves. Pangaea is a realm in which few vampires survive for any length of time, and so developing further Unnatural Affinities for Pangaeans is unlikely but not impossible. Feeding upon these gods of Pangaea is likely to result in something arcane and strange happening to the vampire.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Merits:</b> The following Merits are not available to Neolithic vampires: Status (Clan) or Status (Covenant), and any Merits that use them as prerequisites. The Altar Merit is an exception, which does not require Circle of the Crone Status, instead requiring Occult 3 (as does Cr\u00faac). The use of the Altar Merit is sure to draw the attention of the Strix sooner or later. Dream Visions is available to any vampire with Auspex as a clan Discipline. Kiss of the Succubus likewise becomes available to any vampire with Majesty as a clan Discipline, Pack Alpha becomes available through Animalism, and Unsettling Gaze through Nightmare. City Status is replaced by &quot;Village Status,&quot; functioning largely the same, even if it doesn't cover quite so populous an area. Carthian Law Merits and Invictus Oaths are unavailable.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2022 Clan Banes:</b> Players should work with Storytellers to define a suitable &quot;clan bane&quot; for their character. Any character may use the banes listed in the book as inspiration or as their personal bane. Because of their starting Humanity, all vampire characters spend their unlives with their clan bane active. Storytellers may wish to allow vampires who reach Humanity 10, rare as it is, to shed their clan banes and only regain them if they drop to Humanity 6 or lower again during their unlives.</div> </div> </div> </div>Bump. I know it's not perfect, but it's a start, with Dark Eras out now. Some folks may want to play vampires in the Neolithic, or use them in their chronicles, so having at least a stopgap measure is always good.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Freelancer </b>\u2022 Available for work!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":12}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield","name":"Claire Redfield","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar109_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-29T19:27:27-07:00","id":855434,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Acrozatarim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=710847#post710847\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Nope - it's a really cool piece of writing, but DaveB wrote the sidebar for Other Supernaturals In The Neolithic (tm) and he had less wordcount for the entire thing, all lines, than Claire used just for Vampire there <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> Were I to have had the extra 5k words to include Requiem in the chapter, I think I'd probably tie Kindred into the [REDACTED] that's going on with the Underworld at the time...</div> </div> </div> </div>Hey Chris, wanna tell us more about that flooded Underworld which you would theoretically tie the Kindred to?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-29T21:40:08-07:00","id":855487,"title":"","content":"The Ocean Beyond Life is described pretty well it the chapter. It's the Underworld, but it's a bottomless, endless ocean that seems to be in a cavern. Lately the waters have receded some, and little islands are appearing for ghosts to cling to existence on. The water hasn't stopped getting lower.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Revlid wrote:<br /> Yes, hollowing out your humanity to become an utterly utilitarian asura is the exact suggestion I would expect from you, Aiden.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/168-aiden","name":"Aiden","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-29T22:28:37-07:00","id":855501,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aiden</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=855487#post855487\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Ocean Beyond Life is described pretty well it the chapter. It's the Underworld, but it's a bottomless, endless ocean that seems to be in a cavern. Lately the waters have receded some, and little islands are appearing for ghosts to cling to existence on. The water hasn't stopped getting lower.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm aware, I was curious about having him comment on how he'd connect it to the Kindred. I recall a description of corpses that &quot;cough up brackish water.&quot; But I couldn't tell if that was one of the Bound with a Geist/Cthonian from the Ocean Beyond Life or something to do with Vampires.<br /> <br /> Hence, I'm curious if Chris has any commentary to offer now that he doesn't have to redact anything.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-30T04:04:21-07:00","id":855608,"title":"","content":"So, this is very much unofficial and just my notions as to what I might have proposed if I'd had 5k of Requiem words, but...<br /> <br /> \u200bI would have had at least one primary source of vampires being the retreating waters of the Ocean Beyond Life. As they lower, they reveal strange things on the rocky, barren islands that now rise up from the sea. Perhaps there are strange ceramics, odd pots marked with signs like those of the Vinca yet even older, and when a Sea-Wise exploring the Ocean returns to the World of Life carrying some, he breaks their seals and from them pours forth choking, smoky captured breath that winds its way into the lungs of hapless humans and takes them as puppets even as they are caught on the threshold of death. Perhaps on some of the lifeless shores, bodies are revealed; dead who never sank all the way into the waters and have laid there for centuries, perhaps millenia, in lifeless comas. In the cold air of the Ocean's surface, they find some semblance of sensation returning to their numbed, sodden bodies, and a fierce craving for the heat and fire of life; stumbling confused around the islands, unable to sate their hunger on other dead denizens, some are drawn to Avernian gateways and into the World of Life. Perhaps huge monoliths rear up out of the receding waters, surrounded by lethal, memory-eating brine and their lengths carved with eldritch symbols; as the waters recede, more of the secrets of immortality can be read. Some hubristic Wise, unwilling to see if the monolith's full secrets will be revealed during their lifetimes as the waters slowly drop, decide to try and work rituals only from the passages visible above the waterline; they gain the immortality they crave, in a manner of speaking, as they become conduits, siphons that are allowed a fragment of the life energy they must consume from others to feed into the Ocean Beyond Life.<br /> <br /> And similar notions.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>- Chris Allen, Aberrant Line Developer, Freelance Writer<br /> <br /> \u200bLike my work? Feel like helping me stay supplied with tea? Check out my <b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/acrozatarim\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":14}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1757-acrozatarim","name":"Acrozatarim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-30T07:03:40-07:00","id":855671,"title":"","content":"The Mekhet progenitor, from the clan book, sort of suggest a more lively beginning, from a more lively source.<br /> <br /> 'I wait in a cave; I am cold and alien and before words, and the voices outside show the Tall Ones, who with unfamiliar sounds deliberate over stopping for the night. I hiss and begin to croon. They need only walk into the shadow, my tentacles and blades will grasp them and embrace them and make them my food...'","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-30T08:58:24-07:00","id":855722,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=855671#post855671\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Mekhet progenitor, from the clan book, sort of suggest a more lively beginning, from a more lively source.<br /> <br /> 'I wait in a cave; I am cold and alien and before words, and the voices outside show the Tall Ones, who with unfamiliar sounds deliberate over stopping for the night. I hiss and begin to croon. They need only walk into the shadow, my tentacles and blades will grasp them and embrace them and make them my food...'</div> </div> </div> </div>To me it suggests something that might superficially resemble the beings summoned by the Lasombra mystics in another WoD. (see: Abyssal Mysticism)<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CatDoom</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=734061#post734061\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Funny, I think the last time I read the Black Streets of Babylon section of <i>Ancient Mysteries</i> I must have read &quot;twelve thousand&quot; as &quot;twelve hundred&quot; or something. That is a pretty crazy long time to rule &quot;Babylonia,&quot; especially since there definitely wasn't a Babylon in the 12th millennium BCE. That said, it might be consistent of ancient Mesopotamian historiography, since some of those king lists assign really really long reigns to early kings.</div> </div> </div> </div>I automatically went &quot;he's a king from the ancient Near East, let's divide all the numbers by 10&quot;<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://dl.dropboxusercontent.com/u/7019410/Images/Ex3%20KS%20Badge.gif\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1866-ophion","name":"Ophion","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-30T09:42:51-07:00","id":855749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=855671#post855671\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Mekhet progenitor, from the clan book, sort of suggest a more lively beginning, from a more lively source.<br /> <br /> 'I wait in a cave; I am cold and alien and before words, and the voices outside show the Tall Ones, who with unfamiliar sounds deliberate over stopping for the night. I hiss and begin to croon. They need only walk into the shadow, my tentacles and blades will grasp them and embrace them and make them my food...'</div> </div> </div> </div>The reference to &quot;Tall Ones&quot; and &quot;unfamiliar sounds&quot; suggests to me that the creature in the cave had never encountered Homo sapiens before. I would guess that the events of that vision, and the origin of the Mekhet, took place considerably before the Neolithic era, possibly even on the order of 200,000 years before the appearance of the Vin\u010da culture, depending on where the vision took place and when spoken language developed in the human species.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-31T13:28:31-07:00","id":856688,"title":"","content":"I've already started going research for a Hunters/Dark Fantsy game set in the Neolithic peroid because I don't have the mage book and I don't run werewolf. Anyway...<br /> <br /> Populations that support more than a handful of vampires is not all that crazy in this era. For example if you pop over to neolithic Okoliste (Bosnia) they had 2 to 3 thousand people in their town. That'll easily support 3-5 PC group, not mention hunter gathers, traders and nomad herders who pass through the area. Similar with Sesklo or Vinca-Belo Brdo. You could easily have a game of low level PC vampires serving a big powerful vamp in a river/farming valley and it's town and outlying farming community(s).<br /> <br /> As to vampire themselves, I tend to agree with Clare Redfield in terms of claness vampires. The simplest approach I think would be the accidental embraced out of B&amp;S. Clan specific disciplines require extra xp or IC justification. Banes perhaps are more cultural? Scratch out the no BP gain the bit about waking up each night with 1vitae and I think you're good to go. Depending on where vampires are I would say it probably makes sense to open up some of hte Crone material to them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1031-youvegotwildtalents","name":"youvegotwildtalents","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"80"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-01T10:50:46-07:00","id":857189,"title":"","content":"I've been pondering the Neolithic Vampire question myself, and have come up with a few ideas and points, because I'm considering running a small chat for the big three in the Neolithic, though it doesn't nearly compare with the detail Claire seems to already have gone into. Regardless, here's what I have:<ul><li>Clans don't yet exist. Instead, you have &quot;Birthrights&quot;, that is, the way a given Vampire came into being, which defines ONE of your three &quot;in clan&quot; aka affinity disciplines (Edit: one of which should probably be physical), and also grants a free dot in it, and ONE of the two attribute bonuses you'll get. You then choose two more in-clans and one other Attribute. My ideas so far for birthrights are as follows:<ul><li>Strix-born, those corpses or people that were possessed and later synthesized by a Strix. They start off with Dominate and Manipulation.</li> <li>Spirit-born, those corpses or people that had some heavily sympathetic or physical tie with blood or even actual VItae before being Claimed by a hunger-spirit or blood-spirit, creating something altogether different from a typical Claimed. They start off with Blood Tenebrous and Wits. EDIT: Alternatively, they might simply be the creation of a high ranking blood/death magath's Influences.</li> <li>Horror-born, those that were killed by something they heavily feared, but upon their death received a vision of what some call a &quot;Dark Mother&quot;, who imbued them with their Beast, a primal entity manifested from humanity's fears of hunger and the night. This does imply modern Beasts are right... about SOME Vampires' origins. They start with Nightmare and Presence.</li> <li>Spontaneous Embraces, those that died a bloody, violent death, potentially after already having tasted Vitae (or maybe just human blood), and whose soul was warped by the process, then 'repossessing' their former body. They start with Protean and Stamina.</li> <li>Sired, those few that were actually the childe of another Vampire, with the typical process, rare as it was for them to want to multiply in this time period. They start with two out of three of their affinity disciplines being chosen from those their sire has an affinity for (but only get 1 free dot), as well as both attributes needing to match their Sire's. However, they may immediately gain their sire's bloodline benefit (mentioned below).</li> </ul></li> <li>Banes don't tie into Birthright, you simply choose a Bane appropriate to the character, following the normal Bane rules; this doesn't count towards your normal maximum, though. Spirit-born generally keep the ban or bane of the spirit involved as their own Bane, and Sired usually have one of their sire's banes, but that's not a hard requirement.</li> <li>Humanity starts at 7 as normal, but Kindred don't start off with any Touchstones; they need to expend effort to ground themselves that way (by purchasing the merit). If they lack one entirely, this is counted as all of them being 'Detached', rather than like they've lost all of them, mechanically.</li> <li>At Blood Potency 3, most Vampires learn to refine their blood a bit, coming into their own. They may create a Bloodline at that point (it's much easier in an era when Vampiric nature is still solidifying), with a Gift of the character's design, and a fourth affinity discipline. Bloodlines from the &quot;future&quot; may work as inspiration for the gifts; the fluff won't match up, but it's a good place to start, mechanically, or one can just create an entirely new one from scratch.</li> <li>Sired Vampires may gain their sire's Bloodline at Blood Potency 1 as normal, be Avus'd at 2 by anyone, or may wait until Blood Potency 3 to make their own. On a less mechanical note, 'bloodline' doesn't really exist as a term yet, nor are they individually named by most.</li> <li>Most Vampires have difficulty siring before they have a bloodline, but there's no specific mechanics for that. (Edited)</li> <li><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1nmvnt1UlBBR3DI6_Z1gFyqvPbSQtYi1pp6yIrcowHYM/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Blood Tenebrous</a> is one of the core disciplines, and can be chosen as affinity by anyone; even Vampires were capable of interaction with spirits in an era so heavily tied to them. To note, Shadow gates of course lead into the Border Marches, rather than directly into the Shadow or physical, or into either the physical or shadow realms if already in the border marches.</li> <li>Similarly, any Vampire can learn Cruac (at normal out of clan type costs), though it's not exactly called that yet, and in fact can exist in a different form for any given vampire; some might channel spirits, others might steal the power of the Strix, and others might venerate and beg assistance from Pangean gods. Even blood sorcery is also a bit more fluid at the time; the Themes system from Blood Sorcery is the default assumption, but without the prevention of affecting spiritual realms. However, codified rituals are incredibly rare; most Blood Sorcery is improvised.</li> <li>Theban Sorcery and Mysteries of the Dragon don't yet exist, nor do Oaths. Bloodline-based Sorceries might be possible, though, and Devotions are VERY common.</li> <li>Feeding restrictions are two higher; Vampires of up to Blood Potency 4 can subsist on animals, for instance. Early vampires hadn't yet become so reliant on human blood. There's also no risk of &quot;shadow sickness&quot; if a vampire feeds on spirits, as that's similarly common in the timeframe.</li> </ul><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?affiliate_id=259490\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/themes/stvault/images/affiliatebanner4.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Christopher%20Falco&amp;affiliate_id=259490\" target=\"_blank\">Storytellers' Vault supplements (WoD, CoD)</a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/FalconianP\" target=\"_blank\">My Patreon! (CofD bonuses include: Fae Sorcery/Technomancy for Vampires, Quick Conversion for Inferno, and more!)</a>","edited":{"userid":1130,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1130-falco1029","user":"falco1029","date":". 04-01-2016, 06:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1130-falco1029","name":"falco1029","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1130_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1045"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-02T15:01:18-07:00","id":857949,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>falco1029</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=857189#post857189\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've been pondering the Neolithic Vampire question myself, and have come up with a few ideas and points, because I'm considering running a small chat for the big three in the Neolithic, though it doesn't nearly compare with the detail Claire seems to already have gone into. Regardless, here's what I have:<ul><li>Spontaneous Embraces, those that died a bloody, violent death, potentially after already having tasted Vitae (or maybe just human blood), and whose soul was warped by the process, then 'repossessing' their former body. They start with Protean and Stamina.</li> </ul></div> </div> </div> </div>Can we call these Wendigo? The &quot;tasted blood&quot; idea just make me want to call them Wendigo.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://eoinsstuff.shoutwiki.com/wiki/Genius:_the_Transgression_(2nd_Edition)\" target=\"_blank\">Genius: the Transgression 2E is a thing that's being worked on.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4488-malonkey1","name":"malonkey1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4488_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-02T15:39:34-07:00","id":857966,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>malonkey1</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=857949#post857949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can we call these Wendigo? The &quot;tasted blood&quot; idea just make me want to call them Wendigo.</div> </div> </div> </div>That definitely seems like a fair comparison, especially since I gave them Protean, so they'd often take on monstrous appearances.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?affiliate_id=259490\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/themes/stvault/images/affiliatebanner4.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Christopher%20Falco&amp;affiliate_id=259490\" target=\"_blank\">Storytellers' Vault supplements (WoD, CoD)</a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/FalconianP\" target=\"_blank\">My Patreon! (CofD bonuses include: Fae Sorcery/Technomancy for Vampires, Quick Conversion for Inferno, and more!)</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1130-falco1029","name":"falco1029","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1130_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1045"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-02T19:15:53-07:00","id":858084,"title":"","content":"I've been pondering the issue of stone-age vampires as well, and while I haven't come up with an idea anywhere near as complete as the ones people have proposed here, I do have a bit of purple prose that I'm rather proud of:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We have always been with you.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Wherever brother turned on brother with club or spear or chipped stone ax, we were there to lap up the spilt blood.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Wherever man coveted his neighbors wife, or woman her sister's husband, we were there to whisper cruel encouragements in the night.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Wherever humankind knew death and learned to fear it, we were there to embody that fear.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We thrived in the shadows of your first cities, but before that we laired in deep caves, in burrows beneath the earth, and amidst the tangled branches of forests so thick that they blotted out the hateful sun. When your kind first learned to bury or burn your dead, it was for fear that they might rise to join us in our nightly hunts. You sought succor in empty ritual and superstition, and prayed for the protection of imagined gods, but our hunger would not be denied.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Although we have always been your Kindred, we have not always cleaved to the same five families which rule the modern night. And yet, even in the darkest depths of prehistory, one might find echoes of what we were to become. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Beautiful demons sang beneath the pale moon and lured the unwary toward their serpentine fangs. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Savage spirits rose nightly from the undisturbed earth to stalk foraging bands in the shapes of beasts. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Hungry shadows lurked among the trees while their owners yet slept in defiled graves. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Nightmares in flesh haunted deep caverns and lakes, crooning maddening hymns to the twisted gods that spawned them.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Imperious shades lorded over cowering tribes, demanding toil and sacrifice to feed their bottomless hungers.</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Were these, then, our forbears; the founders of our lines? Perhaps it is so. But the primordial dark held more horrors than these, and many had vanished into memory and myth by the time the first stones of Jericho were laid.</span> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3209,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","user":"CatDoom","date":". 04-02-2016, 10:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-03T08:00:19-07:00","id":858342,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CatDoom</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=858084#post858084\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've been pondering the issue of stone-age vampires as well, and while I haven't come up with an idea anywhere near as complete as the ones people have proposed here, I do have a bit of purple prose that I'm rather proud of:<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>The brother turned on brother part gave me a very interesting idea regarding the Cain myth (borrowed from VtM, admittedly). Longinus was said to have been damned, when he tasted the blood of Jesus of Nazareth on his lips. What if Adam, Eve, and their offspring, were not the first and only humans? Others existed as well, who dealt with creatures of spirit, and one of them was lead to the corpse of Abel (who had been risen to sainthood by his tribe's god) and prompted to partake in Abel's spilled blood?<br /> <br /> Another reading, in line with the gnostic outlook of the Chronicles of Darkness, of the same myth (and one that may have given rise to the Cainite Heresy) would have us examine the Cain and Abel story as a conceptual one. Borrowing from the Kabbalah, Abel can be viewed as one archetype of humanity, while Cain as an entirely different one. Abel is the caregiving altruist, the shepherd who leads his flock and gladly shares with the creator for the sole reason of pleasing Her. Cain is the exact opposite of God's ideal for humanity, the selfishness to desire a &quot;bond with the Creator for one's own fulfillment. [...] Abel has pure desires, the desire to bestow, to receive pleasure from delighting the Creator. It would appear that Cain also has the desire to bestow, but in his case he strives to win the Creator\u2019s favor, to merit His attention, to acquire the spiritual world. He wants to receive all the Light and the infinite pleasure it contains, but only for himself.&quot; (kabbalahblog.info).<br /> <br /> I have often felt drawn to an explanation of Vampires as a distortion and corruption of the gnostic, neoplatonic form of humanity. In this, tragically, it wasn't Cain's act of slaying his brother, that brought about damnation. It was Cain's motives towards winning the Creator's favor, that cursed him to rise from the grave in the way he did. As with most stories in the Kabbalah, Cain and Abel's should be viewed as semi-metaphorical and conceptual. Cain was the first Vampire, only insofar as it is a name given to a specific archetype, form, or energy within creation. In the same vain, Adam and Eve are the two facets of mortal humanity, echoing God's own feminine and masculine aspects. The reason for the subsequent breakdown of each in the material, vis a vis the existence of individuals who may demonstrate more of one archetypal aspect over the other, of individual vampires, and so on, was necessary only so that Humanity's desire to exist and experience individuality (echoing the Hermetic story of the Human archetype <i>falling in love</i> with material Nature) could be fulfilled.<br /> <br /> The Strix, either as a Pangean entity, or simply an archetype without a material representation (and since matter was made by the Creator, without a vessel imbued with Her energies) might have conspired to make the Vampire archetype its own. Either that, or it was created as Vampires distanced themselves from the Divine Energies emanating in the Material, as one possible endpoint for the downward spiral. Perhaps even as a deterrent for vampires who might otherwise forsake their Humanity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":59,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","user":"stylanski","date":". 04-03-2016, 12:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-03T08:13:36-07:00","id":858347,"title":"","content":"That description implies to me, at least, that Vampires and Striges are or were at some point Abyssal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://eoinsstuff.shoutwiki.com/wiki/Genius:_the_Transgression_(2nd_Edition)\" target=\"_blank\">Genius: the Transgression 2E is a thing that's being worked on.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4488-malonkey1","name":"malonkey1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4488_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-03T10:01:46-07:00","id":858386,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>malonkey1</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=858347#post858347\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That description implies to me, at least, that Vampires and Striges are or were at some point Abyssal.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, and it was what was I was hinting towards. I am trying to break this down philosophically. How does each supernatural differ from Humanity?<br /> <br /> <b>Werewolves</b> are part human, part spirit.<br /> <b>Mages</b> are mortals who have reached the level of understanding on the workings of reality, and its manipulation, inherent in all mortals.<br /> They all share a connection to the Supernal, and all humans are theoretically capable of experiencing that yearning (to borrow from Sufism) to awaken, discover, and ascend.<br /> <b>Changelings</b> were forever changed by Arcadia and the True Fae.<br /> <br /> And so on...<br /> <br /> Now, Vampires... What would their place in the world be? What could have possibly spawned them? We could argue that they are parasitic, but this ignores the depth invested elsewhere in CofD, into the mystical symbolism and gnostic imagery behind things mundane and supernatural.<br /> <br /> We can agree that Vampires steal blood from mortals, transforming it into Vitae. Okay, blood. Blood is one of the four humors, symbolizes the element of air, inspires courage, hope, playfulness and so on. I could dwell further into what Air symbolizes in Hermeticism, but that's food for different thoughts. Vampires steal blood to maintain their eternal state of undead existence in the Material.<br /> <br /> The themes and questions relevant here, and excuse my dry, philosophical rumblings, but these are things that I feel need to be considered, if we are to discuss possible origins of Vampires in CofD's world, are theft of life, and <i>why would vampires need to burn Vitae to remain active in the material</i>.<br /> <br /> I feel inclined to believe that early Vampires, similar to Caine before meeting Lilith in VtM, did not possess the breadth of powers they do now. The Disciplines are an extension of the Beast, and the Strix claim that the Beast was <i>their gift</i> to Vampires. So we can at the very least discern that prehistoric, pre-Strix Dealing Kindred vampires, did not demonstrate any Disciplines.<br /> <br /> So what would the early Vampires' purpose and reason of existence be? I guess, and here we can also justify why Vampires tend to be assholes even without the Beast's influence, it was because of mortals who died with blood inside their systems. Coupled with what the deceased considered to be unfinished business (which is almost always selfish in nature, even in the case of protecting a loved one by committing even greater hubris than the persecutors), it provided fertile ground for the mystical transformation of blood to Vitae to fuel the Vampire's undead existence and raise them from the grave. Perhaps a sin committed by the deceased, so vile that it enabled the opposite of a human's positive potential (i.e. awakening to Magic) to come into this world. Another answer could lie in the historical Gnostic group of Cainites, which partly justifies the Sanctified's doctrine as well. Vampires are there to do what needs to be done, just as Judas <i>had</i> to betray Jesus because of a deeper understanding of what was at play. Just like the Cainite gnostic group, Vampires must not refrain from any atrocity, for some greater cosmic good.<br /> <br /> Another question that arises from my trainwreck of thought, what did the Strix get in return? Here, I suspect, is where it matters that the Strix originated from some abyssal, immaterial realm. They bandwagoned Vampires as a means of existing in our realm as well. After all, most non-Material entities in the CofD cosmos have a hard time remaining or returning to realms that are not their own. The Beasts they gifted Vampires with, provided a permanent passageway for them into the Material.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-03T15:53:47-07:00","id":858576,"title":"","content":"Gobekli Tepe...Enoch? The First City?<br /> <br /> A massive hill haunted by the Strix in the Neolithic Period?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe%2C_Urfa.jpg/1280px-G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe%2C_Urfa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe\" target=\"_blank\"> G&ouml;bekli Tepe - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1031-youvegotwildtalents","name":"youvegotwildtalents","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"80"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-03T18:33:56-07:00","id":858658,"title":"","content":"Personally I prefer to keep Judeo-Christian mythology strictly in-character, and the events described the Bible at a remove of at least a few centuries in my CoD headcannon. If I were absolutely forced to answer &quot;does the Christian God exist in the CoD,&quot; my own answer would have to be a resounding no. If there is some kind of supreme deity presiding over the whole mess, then he's not a loving God... in fact, there's a good chance that he's a <i>singularly</i> hateful bastard. That said, I prefer to leave the answer vague.<br /> <br /> More topically, my own idea for stone-age vampires would be for only a few of them to have anything like the &quot;modern&quot; vampire template. These would probably be the misbegotten progeny of the Strix, and would probably have a more limited selection of disciplines, though some sort of Cruac-like blood sorcery wouldn't be out of the question.<br /> <br /> Other proto-vampires would be much different creatures. I really like the idea that the Clans as we known them today came about as a result of some kind of occult convergent evolution or supernatural mimicry, in much the same way that the North Carolina Jiang Shi have grown more &quot;Kindred-like&quot; over time.<br /> <br /> In fact, maybe the &quot;pure&quot; offspring of the Strix are actually not the ancestors of any Clan in particular, but rather a sort of &quot;clanless&quot; line that ultimately died out or assimilated into other groups. That way, every Clan could have a different non-Kindred ancestor that came to intermingle with the Strix-Spawn and eventually became part of the family.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-03T20:01:10-07:00","id":858700,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CatDoom</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=858658#post858658\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally I prefer to keep Judeo-Christian mythology strictly in-character, and the events described the Bible at a remove of at least a few centuries in my CoD headcannon. If I were absolutely forced to answer &quot;does the Christian God exist in the CoD,&quot; my own answer would have to be a resounding no. If there is some kind of supreme deity presiding over the whole mess, then he's not a loving God... in fact, there's a good chance that he's a <i>singularly</i> hateful bastard. That said, I prefer to leave the answer vague.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, but I wasn't implying the supremacy of the monotheistic God or Her and His supremacy over CofD in any way. Just that Vampires, the gothic aesthetic, gargoyles, churches, that sort of thing, plus Gnosticism which is in essence a Christian set of sects, makes this sort of <b><i>mythos</i></b> appropriate. The explanations themselves will be rooted in real-life religion, solely for the sake of good fiction. Just like the Exarchs from Mage are actually gnostic Archons.<br /> <br /> And exactly your belief that God may exist and be a singularly hateful bastard, is an issue central to the questions asked by neoplatonism and gnosticism. Why is good drowned in evil? Why humanity cannot go for more than 40 years of peace and prosperity, and even then it is only for a fragment of the world's population? Gnostics lament the Demiurge's imperfections, Kabbalists try to see the emanations of Divinity in everything, despite its unfortunate coating in imperfect matter. Hermetics strive to rejoice at the small miracles inherent in our otherwise bleak universe. Other neoplatonists believe it is the price we chose to pay in order to experience both Everything and Nothing in the Universe. Evil is good approaching near-zero levels like an asymptote, not an existence independent of good. Personally, I don't have any answers so I wouldn't expect my games to, either. But as I enjoy this sort of fiction and roleplaying, I research them so as to enrich my games with some post-classical Hellenistic philosophy (that is already there to begin with, in both WoD and CofD).<br /> <br /> Edit: Also, the Vampires as a distorted archetype for Humanity, is <i>not</i> an inspiration I took from Judeo-Christianity, but Platonic in outlook (and therefore close to Werewolf and closer to Mage).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":59,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","user":"stylanski","date":". 04-04-2016, 12:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T09:56:23-07:00","id":859082,"title":"","content":"I guess I misunderstood what you were getting at, and I apologize. I am fairly familiar with Gnosticism myself; I've actually been working on a set of sacred documents for a syncretic pseudo-Christian Crone cult, and I've been using some of the surviving Gnostic scriptures as a model. The Gospel of Phillip is actually pretty good for this kind of thing if you pick and choose which sayings you use; it has some oddly cannibalistic imagery, going so far as to say &quot;God is a man-eater.&quot; Fertile ground for a vampiric reinterpretation. :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T10:26:56-07:00","id":859100,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CatDoom</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=859082#post859082\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I guess I misunderstood what you were getting at, and I apologize. I am fairly familiar with Gnosticism myself; I've actually been working on a set of sacred documents for a syncretic pseudo-Christian Crone cult, and I've been using some of the surviving Gnostic scriptures as a model. The Gospel of Phillip is actually pretty good for this kind of thing if you pick and choose which sayings you use; it has some oddly cannibalistic imagery, going so far as to say &quot;God is a man-eater.&quot; Fertile ground for a vampiric reinterpretation. :P</div> </div> </div> </div>No worries, just wanted to make sure I didn't seem like pushing religious views down anyone's throat.<br /> <br /> My current LARP character is a gnostic too, someone who came close to awakening but somehow screwed it up. He's now a Sanctified heretic, who carefully guards a collection of notes on blood sorcery and views God and the Crone as two sides of the same coin (each valid within its own religious context).<br /> <br /> Haven't checked out the Gospel of Phillip, will definitely give it a reading. Thanks for the recommendation!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T17:54:29-07:00","id":860007,"title":"","content":"It might be fun to look at the vampires as mirrors to the societal progress of humanity; some are semi-feral monsters that sleep in caves and kill whenever they hunt, whereas others &quot;farm&quot; humans through either playing at godhood or sheer intimidation factor. You could easily have your circle of Wise or werewolf pack or what have you, and then you come across a village where they offer themselves up to their hungry ancestor every night.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T19:37:59-07:00","id":860058,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=860007#post860007\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It might be fun to look at the vampires as mirrors to the societal progress of humanity; some are semi-feral monsters that sleep in caves and kill whenever they hunt, whereas others &quot;farm&quot; humans through either playing at godhood or sheer intimidation factor. You could easily have your circle of Wise or werewolf pack or what have you, and then you come across a village where they offer themselves up to their hungry ancestor every night.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think tying vampires up with the metaphor of domestication and herds is a pretty savvy way to put them in conversation with the People, kind of like how the Sundered World juxtaposes the rise of agriculture with the fall of Father Wolf and the hunter's paradise.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T15:36:11-07:00","id":860596,"title":"","content":"The big elephant in the room that makes the existence of vampires in the prehistory impossible is that prehistoric people had way way worse health than modern people do. WoD vampires require a shitton of blood to function. You need to spend a blood point just to wake up. You can take half a liter of blood from a modern human without serious consequences. But just a hundred years ago people were fainting all the time due to lack of iron in their diet while at full blood pool. Taking 1 or 2 points from the prehistoric human is debilitating and potentially lethal. There is absolutely no way a caveman tribe can support even a single WoD vampire, much less a coterie.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2347-mahasamatman","name":"Mahasamatman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2347_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"681"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T17:01:10-07:00","id":860649,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mahasamatman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=860596#post860596\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The big elephant in the room that makes the existence of vampires in the prehistory impossible is that prehistoric people had way way worse health than modern people do. WoD vampires require a shitton of blood to function. You need to spend a blood point just to wake up. You can take half a liter of blood from a modern human without serious consequences. But just a hundred years ago people were fainting all the time due to lack of iron in their diet while at full blood pool. Taking 1 or 2 points from the prehistoric human is debilitating and potentially lethal. There is absolutely no way a caveman tribe can support even a single WoD vampire, much less a coterie.</div> </div> </div> </div>This isn't entirely accurate. If you look at hunter gatherer skeletons from around 7 or 6 thousand bc, they can be 2 to 6 inches taller than those farming communities from 5 to 4 thousand bc. People shrank because while they had a steady food supply they were limited to specific types of food. They had plenty of grains, some vegges and some meat while hunter-gatheres ate much wider selection of nuts, meats, berries and wild strains of planets. So to some extent cavemen tribes actually had better diets than your average 14th or 17th century pheasant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1031-youvegotwildtalents","name":"youvegotwildtalents","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"80"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T19:14:21-07:00","id":860720,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>youvegotwildtalents</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/705402-vampires-in-the-neolithic?p=860649#post860649\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They had plenty of grains, some vegges and some meat while hunter-gatheres ate much wider selection of nuts, meats, berries and <b>wild strains of planets</b>. So to some extent cavemen tribes actually had better diets than your average 14th or 17th century <b>pheasant</b>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, if they're eating planets, cavemen are definitely going to outgrow something as small as a pheasant, no question. :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://eoinsstuff.shoutwiki.com/wiki/Genius:_the_Transgression_(2nd_Edition)\" target=\"_blank\">Genius: the Transgression 2E is a thing that's being worked on.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4488-malonkey1","name":"malonkey1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4488_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T19:47:48-07:00","id":860733,"title":"","content":"To add support to the above, Hunter - gatherer societies of the Neolithic tended to be extremely healthy, albeit with a high-risk lifestyle. The first towns, on the other hand, we're unequivocal disasters. Disease could spread more quickly, nutritional deficiency became prevalent as important aspects of the human diet were cut out- the quantity of food and the density of people improved, and that's about it. Everything else took a nosedive for the first couple centuries.<br /> <br /> Also consider the fact that a hunter-gatherer group is engaging in a high-protein, high-fat diet and tons of physical activity. They were very healthy people who lived an extremely hazardous life. Even more so in the CofD, where angry gods, spirits, and who knows what else could come rampaging into the neighbourhood.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}